./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:22:283,"A multimodal framework for assessing the link between pathomics, transcriptomics, and pancreatic cancer mutations.","In Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC), predicting genetic mutations directly from histopathological images using Deep Learning can provide valuable insights. The combination of several omics can provide further knowledge on mechanisms underlying tumor biology. This study aimed at developing an explainable multimodal pipeline to predict genetic mutations for the KRAS, TP53, SMAD4, and CDKN2A genes, integrating pathomic features with transcriptomics from two independent datasets, the TCGA-PAAD, assumed as training set, and the CPTAC-PDA, as external validation set. Large and small configurations of CLAM (Clustering-constrained Attention Multiple Instance Learning) models were evaluated with three different feature extractors (ResNet50, UNI, and CONCH). RNA-seq data were pre-processed both conventionally and using three autoencoder architectures. The processed transcript panels were input into machine learning (ML) models for mutation classification. Attention maps and SHAP were employed, highlighting significant features from both data modalities. A fusion layer or a voting mechanism combined the outputs from pathomic and transcriptomic models, obtaining a multimodal prediction. Performance comparisons were assessed by Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUROC) and Precision-Recall (AUPRC) curves. On the validation set, for KRAS, multimodal ML achieved 0.92 of AUROC and 0.98 of AUPRC. For TP53, the multimodal voting model achieved 0.75 of AUROC and 0.85 of AUPRC. For SMAD4 and CDKN2A, transcriptomic ML models achieved AUROC of 0.71 and 0.65, while multimodal ML showed AUPRC of 0.39 and 0.37, respectively. This approach demonstrated the potential of combining pathomics with transcriptomics, offering an interpretable framework for predicting key genetic mutations in PDAC.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,,,"The abstract describes a multimodal pipeline using existing models (CLAM, ResNet50, UNI, CONCH) and autoencoders for mutation prediction from histopathology and transcriptomics, with no generative text or natural language component. | The abstract describes a multimodal classification pipeline for predicting genetic mutations, not a generative model. It uses pre-trained feature extractors (e.g., UNI, CONCH) and autoencoders for representation, but the core task is predictive classification, not text generation or foundation-model development.","The abstract focuses on a multimodal classification pipeline for mutation prediction using pathomics and transcriptomics; no natural language text is involved, and the models are discriminative, not generative. The paper type is ambiguous between a novel method and an application wrapper, but the absence of a text component is decisive for exclusion."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:25:693,Biologically grounded neocortex computational primitives implemented on neuromorphic hardware improve vision transformer performance,"Significance We implement a biologically grounded cortical circuit motif in neuromorphic hardware and AI architectures to show how experimentally informed neocortical computations, realized through cell-type-specific soft winner-take-all (sWTA) dynamics, can enhance AI. Parvalbumin (PV) provided feedforward inhibition, somatostatin (SST) feedback inhibition, vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) disinhibition, and LAMP5 gain normalization, capturing core mechanisms of cortical gain control and competitive filtering. Hardware implementations demonstrated linear compute-time scaling with load, contrasting the quadratic scaling of simulations. Incorporated as a fixed preprocessing module in Vision Transformers, the motif improved out-of-distribution generalization by ~20% and lowered training cost by enhancing feature selectivity, without requiring additional data or architectural changes. These findings establish a scalable roadmap connecting cortical computation to energy-efficient neuromorphic hardware and robust AI.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EXCLUDE,,,"The abstract describes a biologically inspired module for Vision Transformers, with no text or bio modality (neuroscience only); it is not generative. Scope reviewer's primary_model_paper and architecture reviewer's application_wrapper both conflict, but the record clearly lacks a text component, leading to exclusion under EC2_no_text_component.","The abstract describes a biologically grounded cortical circuit motif used as a fixed preprocessing module in Vision Transformers, indicating an application wrapper; no text or generative components are present, and bio modality is present due to explicit reference to neocortical computations."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:29:927,Computational Design of Synthetic Optical Barcodes in Microdroplets,"Barcodes are useful for identifying objects across time, space, and information modalities. However, materializing and decoding optical and multimodal barcodes on microscopic objects remains difficult despite the increasing need for multiplexed cell analysis. Here, a computational design of randomly combinatorial is presented, yet decodable barcodes in microdroplets. The design is based on a novel Real2Sim2Real framework: it first collects experimental images of optically distinct microparticles, then simulates massive combinatorial images by randomly assembling the imaged particles to train a neural network‐based decoder. It is demonstrated that the decoder, even though trained via simulation, accurately identifies the randomly assembled particles in real hydrogel microdroplets. It also shows that the microdroplets with an additional DNA barcoding functionality are applicable to individually link independently measured microscopic images and transcriptome profiles of pooled single cells.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,,,"The paper describes a neural network decoder for optical barcodes in microdroplets; no biological modality is central to the model, and no text/language component is present. | The abstract presents a decoder trained on simulated images for barcode identification, not a generative architecture. No evidence of generative or foundation-model characteristics.","This paper presents a decoder for optical barcodes without any text/language component; biological data appears only as an application context, not as a central modality of the model. | The abstract describes a decoder for identifying barcodes, not a generative architecture; no mention of autoregressive, diffusion, or other generative mechanism."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:62:496,Multimodal AI for prediction of distant metastasis in carcinoma patients.,"Metastasis of cancer is directly related to death in almost all cases, however a lot is yet to be understood about this process. Despite advancements in the available radiological investigation techniques, not all cases of Distant Metastasis (DM) are diagnosed at initial clinical presentation. Also, there are currently no standard biomarkers of metastasis. Early, accurate diagnosis of DM is however crucial for clinical decision making, and planning of appropriate management strategies. Previous works have achieved little success in attempts to predict DM from either clinical, genomic, radiology, or histopathology data. In this work we attempt a multimodal approach to predict the presence of DM in cancer patients by combining gene expression data, clinical data and histopathology images. We tested a novel combination of Random Forest (RF) algorithm with an optimization technique for gene selection, and investigated if gene expression pattern in the primary tissues of three cancer types (Bladder Carcinoma, Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma, and Head and Neck Squamous Carcinoma) with DM are similar or different. Gene expression biomarkers of DM identified by our proposed method outperformed Differentially Expressed Genes (DEGs) identified by the DESeq2 software package in the task of predicting presence or absence of DM. Genes involved in DM tend to be more cancer type specific rather than general across all cancers. Our results also indicate that multimodal data is more predictive of metastasis than either of the three unimodal data tested, and genomic data provides the highest contribution by a wide margin. The results re-emphasize the importance for availability of sufficient image data when a weakly supervised training technique is used. Code is made available at: https://github.com/rit-cui-lab/Multimodal-AI-for-Prediction-of-Distant-Metastasis-in-Carcinoma-Patients.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,,,"The paper uses gene expression, clinical data, and histopathology images with Random Forest for metastasis prediction; no text/language component is present. | The abstract describes a predictive multimodal system using Random Forest for metastasis classification, not a generative architecture; no evidence of foundation-model pretraining or transferable representations.","The abstract describes a multimodal predictive model using Random Forest, with no mention of text data or generative modeling. The scope reviewer correctly identified no text component; the architecture reviewer's application_wrapper classification is unsupported as no language model is used. Paper type remains unclear as it does not fit any defined category."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:71:1027,The Theranostic Genome,"{'h1': 'Abstract', 'p': ['Theranostic drugs represent an emerging path to deliver on the promise of precision medicine. However, bottlenecks remain in characterizing theranostic targets, identifying theranostic lead compounds, and tailoring theranostic drugs. To overcome these bottlenecks, we present the Theranostic Genome , the part of the human genome whose expression can be utilized to combine therapeutic and diagnostic applications. Using a deep learning-based hybrid human-AI pipeline that cross-references PubMed, the Gene Expression Omnibus, DisGeNET, The Cancer Genome Atlas and the NIH Molecular Imaging and Contrast Agent Database, we bridge individual genes in human cancers with respective theranostic compounds. Cross-referencing the Theranostic Genome with RNAseq data from over 17’000 human tissues identifies theranostic targets and lead compounds for various human cancers, and allows tailoring targeted theranostics to relevant cancer subpopulations. We expect the Theranostic Genome to facilitate the development of new targeted theranostics to better diagnose, understand, treat, and monitor a variety of human cancers.', 'Identifying theranostic compounds for precision medicine remains a difficult task. Here, the authors develop a deep learning-based hybrid human-AI pipeline to integrate and analyse multiple databases and multimodal datasets to establish the Theranostic Genome , which can be used to propose targeted theranostics therapies.']}",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EXCLUDE,,,"The abstract describes a deep learning pipeline for integrating databases to identify theranostic compounds, not a generative model. No generative component is mentioned, and the paper type is unclear.","The abstract describes a deep-learning pipeline that cross-references PubMed (text) with genomic and imaging databases, not a generative foundational model. The scope reviewer's classification as application_wrapper is consistent with the evidence; text component and text-bio bridge are present because PubMed text is used to link genes and compounds."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:78: Gregory Koytiger, Alice M. Walsh, Vaishali Marar, Kayla A. Johnson, Max Highsmith, Alexander R. Abbas, Andrew Stirn, Ariel Brumbaugh, Alex David, Darren Hui, Jeffrey Kahn, Sheng-Yong Niu, Liza J. Ray, Candace Savonen, Stein Setvik, Jeffrey T. Leek, Robert K. Bradley. Generative genomics accurately predicts cancer gene expression [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2026; Part 1 (Regular Abstracts); 2026 Apr 17-22; San Diego, CA. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2026;86(7 Suppl):Abstract nr 4213.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"GEM-1 is a generative genomics model trained on RNA-seq data and uses pretrained LLM embeddings from experimental metadata to condition predictions, establishing a substantive text-bio bridge. | The abstract explicitly describes GEM-1 as a generative genomics framework using a deep latent variable model trained on large-scale transcriptomic data, with evidence of broad pretraining and transferable representations.","The abstract describes GEM-1, a newly developed generative model for predicting gene expression from experimental metadata, making it a primary model paper. Text component is absent in the model's output, but the text-bio bridge is present via metadata harmonization and pretrained embeddings. Scope reviewer's 'application_wrapper' classification is overridden as the paper presents an original model, not a wrapper."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:87:1349,GALAX: Graph-Augmented Language Model for Explainable Reinforcement-Guided Subgraph Reasoning in Precision Medicine,"In precision medicine, quantitative multi-omic features, topological context, and textual biological knowledge play vital roles in identifying disease-critical signaling pathways and targets. Existing pipelines capture only part of these-numerical omics ignore topological context, text-centric LLMs lack quantitative grounded reasoning, and graph-only models underuse node semantics and the generalization of LLMs-limiting mechanistic interpretability. Although Process Reward Models (PRMs) aim to guide reasoning in LLMs, they remain limited by unreliable intermediate evaluation, and vulnerability to reward hacking with computational cost. These gaps motivate integrating quantitative multi-omic signals, topological structure with node annotations, and literature-scale text via LLMs, using subgraph reasoning as the principle bridge linking numeric evidence, topological knowledge and language context. Therefore, we propose GALAX (Graph Augmented LAnguage model with eXplainability), an innovative framework that integrates pretrained Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) into Large Language Models (LLMs) via reinforcement learning guided by a Graph Process Reward Model (GPRM), which generates disease-relevant subgraphs in a step-wise manner initiated by an LLM and iteratively evaluated by a pretrained GNN and schema-based rule check, enabling process-level supervision without explicit labels. As an application, we also introduced Target-QA, a benchmark combining CRISPR-identified targets, multi-omic profiles, and biomedical graph knowledge across diverse cancer cell lines, which enables GNN pretraining for supervising step-wise graph construction and supports long-context reasoning over text-numeric graphs (TNGs), providing a scalable and biologically grounded framework for explainable, reinforcement-guided subgraph reasoning toward reliable and interpretable target discovery in precision medicine.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,INCLUDE,,,"The abstract proposes GALAX, a novel framework integrating GNNs into LLMs for subgraph reasoning, using multi-omic data, topological context, and textual biological knowledge, with explicit text-bio bridge. | The abstract describes a framework for subgraph reasoning using LLMs and GNNs, but the generative output is subgraphs, not text; no evidence of a generative text architecture or foundation-model characteristics for the proposed model.","The abstract proposes GALAX, a novel framework integrating pretrained GNNs into LLMs, clearly describing a new generative model (LLM) for subgraph reasoning, not a wrapper or review. Despite the architecture reviewer's uncertainty, the evidence supports primary model paper and generative status."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:93:                  single-cell biology.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"PGL is a generative model that uses text metadata (tissue, disease) to synthesize single-cell transcriptomes, representing a primary text-bio model. | The abstract introduces PGL, a generative LLM-based model for synthesizing single-cell transcriptomes from metadata, clearly indicating a generative architecture and broad applicability as a foundational tool.","The model uses gene-expression tokens and an LLM to generate single-cell profiles, but no natural-language text component is described; biological tokenization alone does not count as a text component. | The abstract introduces PGL, an LLM-based generative model that synthesizes single-cell RNA-seq profiles from metadata, clearly indicating a generative architecture. It demonstrates broad applicability (e.g., spatial transcriptomics reference, virtual cohorts) and transferable representations, satisfying foundation-model evidence."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:97:1423,Med-PRSIMD: Enhanced Complex Disease Risk Prediction through Integrative Analysis of Multi-Type Data and Medical History Records,"Predicting complex diseases using computational methods have rapidly evolved, which accelerates precision medicine, disease prevention, and so on. In past years, especially after emergence of genomics, major focus was inclined connecting genetic influence to complex diseases wherein many other essential factors such as environment, lifestyles, and so on were constantly neglected. In recent years, researchers have begun predicting disease risk scores by integrating genetic variants data and different lifestyle factors (alcohol intake, etc) and physical parameters (body mass index, age, etc). However, a potential missing link still exists there as most of those methods did not adequately address how to integrate the information from the medical history record that plays a crucial role in complex disease occurrence. In this paper, we have proposed Med-PRSIMD model that integrates genetic variants, lifestyle factors and medical history records to advance complex disease prediction. In this approach, we trained the Transformer model using causal language model pretraining task for extracting medical history records information. We have evaluated the prediction performance of our proposed Med-PRSIMD model on coronary artery disease, type 2 diabetes, and breast cancer patients' data obtained from the UK biobank. Med-PRSIMD achieved state-of-the-art performance compared to existing methods.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,,,"Proposes Med-PRSIMD integrating genetic variants (bio modality) and medical history records using a Transformer with causal language model pretraining, indicating a primary model with a substantive text component and a clear text-bio bridge. | The abstract describes a Transformer model trained with causal language model pretraining, which is a generative (autoregressive) architecture. However, evidence for foundation-model-like broad pretraining or transferable representations is unclear, as the model is evaluated only on three specific disease prediction tasks.","The abstract proposes a new model using causal language model pretraining on medical history records and integrates genetic data, but it is unclear if the records are textual; foundation model evidence is absent, leading to multiple uncertainties."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:98:1378,Modeling and predicting single-cell multi-gene perturbation responses with scLAMBDA,"Understanding cellular responses to genetic perturbations is essential for understanding gene regulation and phenotype formation. While high-throughput single-cell RNA-sequencing has facilitated detailed profiling of heterogeneous transcriptional responses to perturbations at the single-cell level, there remains a pressing need for computational models that can decode the mechanisms driving these responses and accurately predict outcomes to prioritize target genes for experimental design. Here, we present scLAMBDA, a deep generative learning framework designed to model and predict single-cell transcriptional responses to genetic perturbations, including single-gene and combinatorial multi-gene perturbations. By leveraging gene embeddings derived from large language models, scLAMBDA effectively integrates prior biological knowledge and disentangles basal cell states from perturbation-specific salient representations. Through comprehensive evaluations on multiple single-cell CRISPR Perturb-seq datasets, scLAMBDA consistently outperformed state-of-the-art methods in predicting perturbation outcomes, achieving higher prediction accuracy. Notably, scLAMBDA demonstrated robust generalization to unseen target genes and perturbations, and its predictions captured both average expression changes and the heterogeneity of single-cell responses. Furthermore, its predictions enable diverse downstream analyses, including the identification of differentially expressed genes and the exploration of genetic interactions, demonstrating its utility and versatility.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,INCLUDE,,,"The paper introduces scLAMBDA, a deep generative model for single-cell transcriptional responses, which uses gene embeddings from LLMs as input but does not include a natural-language text component within the model itself. | The abstract presents scLAMBDA as a deep generative learning framework for modeling perturbation responses, explicitly stating generative architecture and demonstrating generalization and broad applicability, indicating foundation-model-like characteristics.","The abstract describes scLAMBDA as a novel deep generative framework that uses LLM-derived gene embeddings to model single-cell perturbation responses, indicating a primary model paper with a clear text-bio bridge. The scope reviewer's application_wrapper classification is rejected because the paper presents a new model, not just a wrapper."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:111:                This work constitutes an interim report from the initial sprint of our Multi-Modal AI Gym for Science (MMAI), an initiative dedicated to building foundation models for drug discovery and aging research.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,INCLUDE,,,"The abstract presents Longevity-LLM v0.1 fine-tuned on DNA methylation, proteomics, and other biological data for age prediction and proteomic profile generation, but no natural-language text component is described; the model operates solely on biological modalities. | The abstract introduces Longevity-LLM, a fine-tuned Qwen3-14B model that performs generative tasks such as proteomic profile generation and age prediction, indicating a generative architecture. It also shows foundation-model-like characteristics by leveraging a pretrained LLM and demonstrating broad applicability across modalities.","The abstract describes Longevity-LLM, a fine-tuned Qwen3-14B (foundation LLM) on multiple bio data types, performing generative tasks like age prediction and proteomic profile generation, confirming all criteria."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:137:241,sciLaMA: A Single-Cell Representation Learning Framework to Leverage Prior Knowledge from Large Language Models.,"Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) enables high-resolution exploration of cellular diversity and gene regulation, yet analyzing such data remains challenging due to technical and methodological limitations. Existing task-specific deep generative models like Variational Auto-Encoder (VAE) and its variants struggle to incorporate external biological knowledge, while transformer-based foundational large Language Models (LLMs or large LaMs) face limitations in computational cost and applicability to tabular gene expression data. Here, we introduce sciL-aMA (single-cell interpretable Language Model Adapter), a novel representation learning frame-work that bridges these gaps by integrating static gene embeddings from multimodal LLMs with scRNA-seq tabular data through a paired-VAE architecture. Our approach generates context-aware representations for both cells and genes and outperforms state-of-the-art methods in key single-cell downstream tasks, including batch effect correction, cell clustering, and cell-state-specific gene marker and module identification, while maintaining computational efficiency. sciL-aMA offers a computationally efficient, unified framework for comprehensive single-cell data analysis and biologically interpretable gene module discovery. Source code is available at https://github.com/microsoft/sciLaMA",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"The abstract describes a novel paired-VAE architecture that integrates static gene embeddings from multimodal LLMs with scRNA-seq data, constituting a primary model paper with a clear text-bio bridge (LLM embeddings used for gene representation) and generative model (VAE). The scope reviewer's application_wrapper classification is overruled because the paper introduces a new framework, not merely wrapping an existing LLM.","The proposed sciL-aMA model uses a paired-VAE for single-cell data and integrates static gene embeddings from LLMs, but does not itself process or generate text; thus it lacks a text component, leading to exclusion."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:3:benchmark:2,Cellwhisperer: Wenn Forscher mit Zellen chatten,"Wiener Bioinformatiker haben ein großes Sprachmodell entwickelt, mit dem Forscher in natürlicher Sprache chatten können. Sie verwenden das KI-System für die Auswertung riesiger Datenmengen aus der Einzelzellforschung.",benchmark_boundary_case,Cellwhisperer: Wenn Forscher mit Zellen chatten,"Wiener Bioinformatiker haben ein großes Sprachmodell entwickelt, mit dem Forscher in natürlicher Sprache chatten können. Sie verwenden das KI-System für die Auswertung riesiger Datenmengen aus der Einzelzellforschung.",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,unclear,,,,,"Presents a new large language model, Cellwhisperer, for natural-language chat to analyze single-cell data, clearly meeting all criteria. | Abstract describes a large language model for natural language chat applied to single-cell data; generative model present (chat) but foundation model evidence unclear due to lack of detail on pretraining or transferability.","Presents a new large language model, Cellwhisperer, for natural-language chat to analyze single-cell data, clearly meeting all criteria.",Abstract describes a large language model for natural language chat applied to single-cell data; generative model present (chat) but foundation model evidence unclear due to lack of detail on pretraining or transferability.,,Cellwhisperer: Wenn Forscher mit Zellen chatten,"Wiener Bioinformatiker haben ein großes Sprachmodell entwickelt, mit dem Forscher in natürlicher Sprache chatten können. Sie verwenden das KI-System für die Auswertung riesiger Datenmengen aus der Einzelzellforschung.",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,unclear,,,,,"The abstract presents a new large language model (Cellwhisperer) for natural-language chat with researchers, applied to single-cell data analysis, satisfying all criteria. | Abstract describes development of a large language model for chatting about single-cell data, indicating generative capability but lacks detail on pretraining or architecture.","The abstract presents a new large language model (Cellwhisperer) for natural-language chat with researchers, applied to single-cell data analysis, satisfying all criteria.","Abstract describes development of a large language model for chatting about single-cell data, indicating generative capability but lacks detail on pretraining or architecture.",,Cellwhisperer: Wenn Forscher mit Zellen chatten,"Wiener Bioinformatiker haben ein großes Sprachmodell entwickelt, mit dem Forscher in natürlicher Sprache chatten können. Sie verwenden das KI-System für die Auswertung riesiger Datenmengen aus der Einzelzellforschung.",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,unclear,,,,,"The abstract presents a new large language model enabling natural-language chat for single-cell data analysis, clearly meeting all criteria. | The abstract describes a large language model developed for chatting about single-cell data, indicating a generative model. However, the abstract is too brief to confirm foundation-model characteristics like broad pretraining.","The abstract presents a new large language model enabling natural-language chat for single-cell data analysis, clearly meeting all criteria.","The abstract describes a large language model developed for chatting about single-cell data, indicating a generative model. However, the abstract is too brief to confirm foundation-model characteristics like broad pretraining.",,Cellwhisperer: Wenn Forscher mit Zellen chatten,"Wiener Bioinformatiker haben ein großes Sprachmodell entwickelt, mit dem Forscher in natürlicher Sprache chatten können. Sie verwenden das KI-System für die Auswertung riesiger Datenmengen aus der Einzelzellforschung.",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces a new large language model that enables natural‑language chat with researchers and is used to analyze single‑cell data, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria. | The abstract reports a large language model that enables researchers to chat in natural language about single‑cell data, indicating a generative decoder architecture and foundation‑model scale.","The abstract introduces a new large language model that enables natural‑language chat with researchers and is used to analyze single‑cell data, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria.","The abstract reports a large language model that enables researchers to chat in natural language about single‑cell data, indicating a generative decoder architecture and foundation‑model scale.",,Cellwhisperer: Wenn Forscher mit Zellen chatten,"Wiener Bioinformatiker haben ein großes Sprachmodell entwickelt, mit dem Forscher in natürlicher Sprache chatten können. Sie verwenden das KI-System für die Auswertung riesiger Datenmengen aus der Einzelzellforschung.",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents a new large language model (Cellwhisperer) that enables natural‑language chat with researchers and is applied to analyze single‑cell data, satisfying all eligibility criteria. | The abstract describes a large language model that enables natural‑language chat and is applied to single‑cell data analysis, indicating a generative decoder architecture and foundation‑model‑like broad applicability.","The abstract presents a new large language model (Cellwhisperer) that enables natural‑language chat with researchers and is applied to analyze single‑cell data, satisfying all eligibility criteria.","The abstract describes a large language model that enables natural‑language chat and is applied to single‑cell data analysis, indicating a generative decoder architecture and foundation‑model‑like broad applicability.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:12:benchmark:5,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,unclear,no,no,,,,,"GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tools to answer genomics questions, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model. | GeneGPT is a method that augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI APIs, not a new generative model. The paper does not propose a generative architecture or foundation model; it is an application wrapper.","GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tools to answer genomics questions, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","GeneGPT is a method that augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI APIs, not a new generative model. The paper does not propose a generative architecture or foundation model; it is an application wrapper.",,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,,,,,"The paper augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tools for question answering, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model. | GeneGPT is a method that augments an existing generative LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs for genomics question answering; it does not introduce a new generative model or foundation model, making it an application wrapper.","The paper augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tools for question answering, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","GeneGPT is a method that augments an existing generative LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs for genomics question answering; it does not introduce a new generative model or foundation model, making it an application wrapper.",,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,,,,,"GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs to answer genomics questions, which is an application wrapper around an external LLM, not a primary generative text-bio foundational model. | The paper describes GeneGPT as a method that augments an existing generative LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs for genomics question answering, which is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs to answer genomics questions, which is an application wrapper around an external LLM, not a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","The paper describes GeneGPT as a method that augments an existing generative LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs for genomics question answering, which is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model.",,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,yes,yes,answering genomics questions,none,augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes GeneGPT as a method that augments an existing LLM (Codex) with API calls to answer genomics questions, without introducing a new multimodal generative model. Hence it is a wrapper of a foundation model, and the text‑bio bridge is limited to tool use rather than a central multimodal model.","The abstract presents GeneGPT as a new method that augments an LLM with NCBI API calls to answer genomics questions, indicating a primary model with both a text component and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The paper augments an existing LLM (Codex) with a decoding algorithm to invoke NCBI APIs, constituting a wrapper around a pre‑trained model rather than introducing a new primary generative model.","The abstract describes GeneGPT as a method that augments an existing LLM (Codex) with API calls to answer genomics questions, without introducing a new multimodal generative model. Hence it is a wrapper of a foundation model, and the text‑bio bridge is limited to tool use rather than a central multimodal model.",GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes GeneGPT, a novel method that augments an LLM with NCBI API calls to answer genomics questions, indicating a substantive natural‑language component and a direct text‑to‑biology bridge centered on genomic data. | The abstract presents GeneGPT with an augmented decoding algorithm that generates answers and triggers API calls, confirming a generative architecture, and leverages a large language model across eight biomedical tasks, indicating foundation-model-like breadth.","The abstract describes GeneGPT, a novel method that augments an LLM with NCBI API calls to answer genomics questions, indicating a substantive natural‑language component and a direct text‑to‑biology bridge centered on genomic data.","The abstract presents GeneGPT with an augmented decoding algorithm that generates answers and triggers API calls, confirming a generative architecture, and leverages a large language model across eight biomedical tasks, indicating foundation-model-like breadth.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:13:benchmark:4,GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,yes,uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"GenePT uses frozen ChatGPT embeddings for downstream classification tasks, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model. No generative component is described.","GenePT uses frozen GPT-3.5 embeddings from NCBI text descriptions as features for downstream classification tasks, fitting the definition of an application wrapper rather than a primary text-bio model.","GenePT uses ChatGPT embeddings to produce gene and cell embeddings, but does not itself implement a generative architecture; it is an embedding-based method, not a generative model.","GenePT uses frozen ChatGPT embeddings for downstream classification tasks, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model. No generative component is described.",GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,yes,no,yes,,,,,"GenePT uses external LLM (ChatGPT) embeddings from text descriptions as features for downstream biological tasks, without presenting a new multimodal generative model. This qualifies as an application wrapper around an existing LLM. | GenePT leverages precomputed GPT-3.5 embeddings to create cell representations for classification tasks; no new generative model is proposed, and the work is an application wrapper around an existing generative model.","GenePT uses external LLM (ChatGPT) embeddings from text descriptions as features for downstream biological tasks, without presenting a new multimodal generative model. This qualifies as an application wrapper around an existing LLM.","GenePT leverages precomputed GPT-3.5 embeddings to create cell representations for classification tasks; no new generative model is proposed, and the work is an application wrapper around an existing generative model.",,GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,no,yes,,,,,"GenePT is a primary model that uses ChatGPT embeddings of gene text descriptions to build single-cell embeddings, satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria. | GenePT uses precomputed ChatGPT embeddings for gene and cell representation; it does not describe a generative architecture for GenePT itself, and downstream tasks are classification, not generation.","GenePT is a primary model that uses ChatGPT embeddings of gene text descriptions to build single-cell embeddings, satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria.","GenePT uses precomputed ChatGPT embeddings for gene and cell representation; it does not describe a generative architecture for GenePT itself, and downstream tasks are classification, not generation.",,GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,yes,NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings,uses ChatGPT embeddings of genes to generate single-cell embeddings by averaging weighted gene embeddings,generates single-cell embeddings by averaging gene embeddings weighted by expression level,wrapper_boundary,"GenePT leverages existing ChatGPT embeddings of gene literature to produce cell embeddings without training a new generative model, fitting the definition of an application wrapper; the text–bio interaction is limited to using text-derived embeddings as features, not a true multimodal generative bridge.","The paper proposes GenePT, which relies on ChatGPT‑derived gene embeddings to build cell representations; this is a wrapper around an external LLM rather than a new primary generative model.","The abstract introduces GenePT, a new model that builds gene and cell embeddings using ChatGPT-generated embeddings, with broad downstream evaluation. No decoder or explicit generative mechanism is described, so it is not a generative model.","GenePT leverages existing ChatGPT embeddings of gene literature to produce cell embeddings without training a new generative model, fitting the definition of an application wrapper; the text–bio interaction is limited to using text-derived embeddings as features, not a true multimodal generative bridge.",GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,yes,NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract reports using ChatGPT embeddings of gene literature to create gene and cell embeddings via averaging, without training a new generative model. This constitutes a wrapper around an existing LLM, so the paper is classified as an application wrapper and lacks a generative component.","The work introduces GenePT, a new method that leverages ChatGPT‑derived gene text embeddings to construct cell embeddings, qualifying as an application wrapper that uses an external LLM; it clearly involves gene expression data (biological modality) and natural‑language gene descriptions (text component) forming a text‑bio bridge.","GenePT introduces a new model that creates gene and cell embeddings using ChatGPT, but the abstract does not describe a decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism.","The abstract reports using ChatGPT embeddings of gene literature to create gene and cell embeddings via averaging, without training a new generative model. This constitutes a wrapper around an existing LLM, so the paper is classified as an application wrapper and lacks a generative component."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:37:                  T cells from multiple donors, and outperforms all baselines after self-supervised test-time adaptation. These results demonstrate that coordinated scaling of causal perturbation data and model capacity yields foundation models capable of generalizable perturbation prediction across cellular contexts, with potential applications for improving computational target identification, validation, and context-specific therapeutic prioritization.",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"Presents X-Cell, a diffusion language model that predicts perturbation responses using cross-attention to natural-language-derived priors, satisfying text component and text-bio bridge. | The paper introduces X-Cell, a diffusion language model for causal perturbation prediction, showing generative diffusion mechanism and foundation-model scaling. However, the output is biological (gene expression changes) rather than text, placing it at the biological token output boundary.","Presents X-Cell, a diffusion language model that predicts perturbation responses using cross-attention to natural-language-derived priors, satisfying text component and text-bio bridge.","The paper introduces X-Cell, a diffusion language model for causal perturbation prediction, showing generative diffusion mechanism and foundation-model scaling. However, the output is biological (gene expression changes) rather than text, placing it at the biological token output boundary.",,X-Cell: Scaling Causal Perturbation Prediction Across Diverse Cellular Contexts via Diffusion Language Models,"Abstract
