achine learning (ML) models for mutation classification. Attention maps and SHAP were employed, highlighting significant features from both data modalities. A fusion layer or a voting mechanism combined the outputs from pathomic and transcriptomic models, obtaining a multimodal prediction. Performance comparisons were assessed by Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUROC) and Precision-Recall (AUPRC) curves. On the validation set, for KRAS, multimodal ML achieved 0.92 of AUROC and 0.98 of AUPRC. For TP53, the multimodal voting model achieved 0.75 of AUROC and 0.85 of AUPRC. For SMAD4 and CDKN2A, transcriptomic ML models achieved AUROC of 0.71 and 0.65, while multimodal ML showed AUPRC of 0.39 and 0.37, respectively. This approach demonstrated the potential of combining pathomics with transcriptomics, offering an interpretable framework for predicting key genetic mutations in PDAC.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a multimodal classification pipeline for mutation prediction using pathomics and transcriptomics, with no natural-language text component or generative mechanism; thus excluded under EC2_no_text_component.","The abstract describes a multimodal pipeline combining pathomics and transcriptomics for mutation prediction; no text/language component is present, so it does not meet the text component criterion.","The abstract describes a multimodal pipeline for mutation classification using feature extractors and ML models; no generative mechanism is mentioned, and the work is an application of existing models, not a primary generative text-bio foundation model.","The abstract describes a multimodal classification pipeline for mutation prediction using pathomics and transcriptomics, with no natural-language text component or generative mechanism; thus excluded under EC2_no_text_component.","A multimodal framework for assessing the link between pathomics, transcriptomics, and pancreatic cancer mutations.","In Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC), predicting genetic mutations directly from histopathological images using Deep Learning can provide valuable insights. The combination of several omics can provide further knowledge on mechanisms underlying tumor biology. This study aimed at developing an explainable multimodal pipeline to predict genetic mutations for the KRAS, TP53, SMAD4, and CDKN2A genes, integrating pathomic features with transcriptomics from two independent datasets, the TCGA-PAAD, assumed as training set, and the CPTAC-PDA, as external validation set. Large and small configurations of CLAM (Clustering-constrained Attention Multiple Instance Learning) models were evaluated with three different feature extractors (ResNet50, UNI, and CONCH). RNA-seq data were pre-processed both conventionally and using three autoencoder architectures. The processed transcript panels were input into machine learning (ML) models for mutation classification. Attention maps and SHAP were employed, highlighting significant features from both data modalities. A fusion layer or a voting mechanism combined the outputs from pathomic and transcriptomic models, obtaining a multimodal prediction. Performance comparisons were assessed by Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUROC) and Precision-Recall (AUPRC) curves. On the validation set, for KRAS, multimodal ML achieved 0.92 of AUROC and 0.98 of AUPRC. For TP53, the multimodal voting model achieved 0.75 of AUROC and 0.85 of AUPRC. For SMAD4 and CDKN2A, transcriptomic ML models achieved AUROC of 0.71 and 0.65, while multimodal ML showed AUPRC of 0.39 and 0.37, respectively. This approach demonstrated the potential of combining pathomics with transcriptomics, offering an interpretable framework for predicting key genetic mutations in PDAC.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract presents a multimodal pipeline using existing feature extractors (ResNet50, UNI, CONCH) and ML models for mutation classification; no natural-language text or generative mechanism is described, so it is an application wrapper.","The abstract describes a multimodal pipeline combining pathomics and transcriptomics for mutation classification; no natural-language text component is mentioned, so it fails the text component criterion.","The paper presents a multimodal pipeline for mutation classification using existing feature extractors and ML models; no generative mechanism is described, and the contribution is a task-specific application wrapper.","The abstract presents a multimodal pipeline using existing feature extractors (ResNet50, UNI, CONCH) and ML models for mutation classification; no natural-language text or generative mechanism is described, so it is an application wrapper.","A multimodal framework for assessing the link between pathomics, transcriptomics, and pancreatic cancer mutations.","In Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC), predicting genetic mutations directly from histopathological images using Deep Learning can provide valuable insights. The combination of several omics can provide further knowledge on mechanisms underlying tumor biology. This study aimed at developing an explainable multimodal pipeline to predict genetic mutations for the KRAS, TP53, SMAD4, and CDKN2A genes, integrating pathomic features with transcriptomics from two independent datasets, the TCGA-PAAD, assumed as training set, and the CPTAC-PDA, as external validation set. Large and small configurations of CLAM (Clustering-constrained Attention Multiple Instance Learning) models were evaluated with three different feature extractors (ResNet50, UNI, and CONCH). RNA-seq data were pre-processed both conventionally and using three autoencoder architectures. The processed transcript panels were input into machine learning (ML) models for mutation classification. Attention maps and SHAP were employed, highlighting significant features from both data modalities. A fusion layer or a voting mechanism combined the outputs from pathomic and transcriptomic models, obtaining a multimodal prediction. Performance comparisons were assessed by Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUROC) and Precision-Recall (AUPRC) curves. On the validation set, for KRAS, multimodal ML achieved 0.92 of AUROC and 0.98 of AUPRC. For TP53, the multimodal voting model achieved 0.75 of AUROC and 0.85 of AUPRC. For SMAD4 and CDKN2A, transcriptomic ML models achieved AUROC of 0.71 and 0.65, while multimodal ML showed AUPRC of 0.39 and 0.37, respectively. This approach demonstrated the potential of combining pathomics with transcriptomics, offering an interpretable framework for predicting key genetic mutations in PDAC.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes only image (pathomics) and transcriptomic data with ML classifiers; there is no mention of natural‑language text, text‑bio alignment, or generative mechanisms, thus it fails the text component criterion and is excluded.","The abstract describes only image (pathomics) and transcriptomic data with no mention of natural-language text, captions, or prompts; therefore no text component or bridge is present.","The abstract focuses on predicting genetic mutations using CLAM, ResNet50, and autoencoders, with no generative mechanism described; it is an application pipeline rather than a new generative model.","The abstract describes only image (pathomics) and transcriptomic data with ML classifiers; there is no mention of natural‑language text, text‑bio alignment, or generative mechanisms, thus it fails the text component criterion and is excluded.","A multimodal framework for assessing the link between pathomics, transcriptomics, and pancreatic cancer mutations.","In Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC), predicting genetic mutations directly from histopathological images using Deep Learning can provide valuable insights. The combination of several omics can provide further knowledge on mechanisms underlying tumor biology. This study aimed at developing an explainable multimodal pipeline to predict genetic mutations for the KRAS, TP53, SMAD4, and CDKN2A genes, integrating pathomic features with transcriptomics from two independent datasets, the TCGA-PAAD, assumed as training set, and the CPTAC-PDA, as external validation set. Large and small configurations of CLAM (Clustering-constrained Attention Multiple Instance Learning) models were evaluated with three different feature extractors (ResNet50, UNI, and CONCH). RNA-seq data were pre-processed both conventionally and using three autoencoder architectures. The processed transcript panels were input into machine learning (ML) models for mutation classification. Attention maps and SHAP were employed, highlighting significant features from both data modalities. A fusion layer or a voting mechanism combined the outputs from pathomic and transcriptomic models, obtaining a multimodal prediction. Performance comparisons were assessed by Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUROC) and Precision-Recall (AUPRC) curves. On the validation set, for KRAS, multimodal ML achieved 0.92 of AUROC and 0.98 of AUPRC. For TP53, the multimodal voting model achieved 0.75 of AUROC and 0.85 of AUPRC. For SMAD4 and CDKN2A, transcriptomic ML models achieved AUROC of 0.71 and 0.65, while multimodal ML showed AUPRC of 0.39 and 0.37, respectively. This approach demonstrated the potential of combining pathomics with transcriptomics, offering an interpretable framework for predicting key genetic mutations in PDAC.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,no natural-language text component described,no text‑bio alignment described,performing mutation classification (no generative mechanism),clear_exclude,"The abstract details a multimodal predictive pipeline using pathomics and transcriptomics but lacks any natural‑language text component or generative mechanism, thus it is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.","The abstract describes a multimodal pipeline integrating pathology images and transcriptomic data to predict mutations, with no mention of natural-language text or generative modeling, so it is an application wrapper rather than a primary text‑bio model.",The abstract describes mutation classification using multimodal ML models and provides no evidence of a generative architecture.,"The abstract details a multimodal predictive pipeline using pathomics and transcriptomics but lacks any natural‑language text component or generative mechanism, thus it is excluded under EC2_no_text_component."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:58:1198,Big Bird: Transformers for Longer Sequences,"Transformers-based models, such as BERT, have been one of the most successful deep learning models for NLP. Unfortunately, one of their core limitations is the quadratic dependency (mainly in terms of memory) on the sequence length due to their full attention mechanism. To remedy this, we propose, BigBird, a sparse attention mechanism that reduces this quadratic dependency to linear. We show that BigBird is a universal approximator of sequence functions and is Turing complete, thereby preserving these properties of the quadratic, full attention model. Along the way, our theoretical analysis reveals some of the benefits of having $O(1)$ global tokens (such as CLS), that attend to the entire sequence as part of the sparse attention mechanism. The proposed sparse attention can handle sequences of length up to 8x of what was previously possible using similar hardware. As a consequence of the capability to handle longer context, BigBird drastically improves performance on various NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization. We also propose novel applications to genomics data.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Big Bird: Transformers for Longer Sequences,"Transformers-based models, such as BERT, have been one of the most successful deep learning models for NLP. Unfortunately, one of their core limitations is the quadratic dependency (mainly in terms of memory) on the sequence length due to their full attention mechanism. To remedy this, we propose, BigBird, a sparse attention mechanism that reduces this quadratic dependency to linear. We show that BigBird is a universal approximator of sequence functions and is Turing complete, thereby preserving these properties of the quadratic, full attention model. Along the way, our theoretical analysis reveals some of the benefits of having $O(1)$ global tokens (such as CLS), that attend to the entire sequence as part of the sparse attention mechanism. The proposed sparse attention can handle sequences of length up to 8x of what was previously possible using similar hardware. As a consequence of the capability to handle longer context, BigBird drastically improves performance on various NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization. We also propose novel applications to genomics data.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge,bio_modality_unclear,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,unclear,,,,,"The abstract presents BigBird as a general NLP transformer improvement with a mention of genomics applications but no central biological modality or text-bio bridge; bio modality is unclear from the abstract. | The abstract describes an encoder-only sparse attention transformer (BigBird) for longer sequences, referencing BERT and tasks like question answering and summarization, but does not mention any generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion. Therefore it is not a generative model.",The abstract presents BigBird as a general NLP transformer improvement with a mention of genomics applications but no central biological modality or text-bio bridge; bio modality is unclear from the abstract.,"The abstract describes an encoder-only sparse attention transformer (BigBird) for longer sequences, referencing BERT and tasks like question answering and summarization, but does not mention any generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion. Therefore it is not a generative model.",,Big Bird: Transformers for Longer Sequences,"Transformers-based models, such as BERT, have been one of the most successful deep learning models for NLP. Unfortunately, one of their core limitations is the quadratic dependency (mainly in terms of memory) on the sequence length due to their full attention mechanism. To remedy this, we propose, BigBird, a sparse attention mechanism that reduces this quadratic dependency to linear. We show that BigBird is a universal approximator of sequence functions and is Turing complete, thereby preserving these properties of the quadratic, full attention model. Along the way, our theoretical analysis reveals some of the benefits of having $O(1)$ global tokens (such as CLS), that attend to the entire sequence as part of the sparse attention mechanism. The proposed sparse attention can handle sequences of length up to 8x of what was previously possible using similar hardware. As a consequence of the capability to handle longer context, BigBird drastically improves performance on various NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization. We also propose novel applications to genomics data.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC1_no_bio_modality,,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization,none,none,clear_exclude,Abstract presents a transformer architecture for NLP without biological modality or explicit generative mechanism. Excluded under EC1_no_bio_modality.,"BigBird is a general transformer model for longer sequences, applied to NLP tasks and genomics. Biological modality is not central, and no text-bio bridge is described.","Proposes BigBird sparse attention for longer sequences; does not explicitly describe a generative mechanism or pretraining, making generative and FM evidence unclear.",Abstract presents a transformer architecture for NLP without biological modality or explicit generative mechanism. Excluded under EC1_no_bio_modality.,Big Bird: Transformers for Longer Sequences,"Transformers-based models, such as BERT, have been one of the most successful deep learning models for NLP. Unfortunately, one of their core limitations is the quadratic dependency (mainly in terms of memory) on the sequence length due to their full attention mechanism. To remedy this, we propose, BigBird, a sparse attention mechanism that reduces this quadratic dependency to linear. We show that BigBird is a universal approximator of sequence functions and is Turing complete, thereby preserving these properties of the quadratic, full attention model. Along the way, our theoretical analysis reveals some of the benefits of having $O(1)$ global tokens (such as CLS), that attend to the entire sequence as part of the sparse attention mechanism. The proposed sparse attention can handle sequences of length up to 8x of what was previously possible using similar hardware. As a consequence of the capability to handle longer context, BigBird drastically improves performance on various NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization. We also propose novel applications to genomics data.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,,bio_modality_unclear,primary_model_paper,yes,no,unclear,unclear,NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization,none,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"Text component is clear from NLP tasks, but bio modality is only hinted by 'genomics data' without specification, no text-bio bridge described, and generative status is unclear as the model is an attention mechanism not explicitly generative.","The abstract presents a new transformer architecture (BigBird) for longer sequences with clear NLP applications, but only vaguely mentions 'novel applications to genomics data' without specifying a text-bio bridge or generative modeling, making bio modality and text-bio bridge unclear.","The abstract proposes a sparse attention mechanism for transformers, improving long sequence handling. It does not explicitly describe a generative architecture or mechanism; tasks like summarization are mentioned but not tied to model generation. Generative status is unclear.","Text component is clear from NLP tasks, but bio modality is only hinted by 'genomics data' without specification, no text-bio bridge described, and generative status is unclear as the model is an attention mechanism not explicitly generative.",Big Bird: Transformers for Longer Sequences,"Transformers-based models, such as BERT, have been one of the most successful deep learning models for NLP. Unfortunately, one of their core limitations is the quadratic dependency (mainly in terms of memory) on the sequence length due to their full attention mechanism. To remedy this, we propose, BigBird, a sparse attention mechanism that reduces this quadratic dependency to linear. We show that BigBird is a universal approximator of sequence functions and is Turing complete, thereby preserving these properties of the quadratic, full attention model. Along the way, our theoretical analysis reveals some of the benefits of having $O(1)$ global tokens (such as CLS), that attend to the entire sequence as part of the sparse attention mechanism. The proposed sparse attention can handle sequences of length up to 8x of what was previously possible using similar hardware. As a consequence of the capability to handle longer context, BigBird drastically improves performance on various NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization. We also propose novel applications to genomics data.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge,,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,improves performance on various NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization,none,none,clear_include,"BigBird introduces a new transformer architecture (primary model) with NLP tasks (text component) and mentions genomics applications (bio modality), but provides no explicit text‑bio integration or generative decoder, so it is included as a primary model without a text‑bio bridge or generative component.","The abstract introduces a new transformer model (BigBird) with NLP tasks and mentions genomics applications, but it does not describe a substantive text‑bio mapping or bridge.","BigBird is presented as a transformer architecture for longer sequences with broad NLP performance and genomics applications, but the abstract does not explicitly describe a generative decoder or autoregressive mechanism, making generative status unclear while still showing foundation-model-like breadth.","BigBird introduces a new transformer architecture (primary model) with NLP tasks (text component) and mentions genomics applications (bio modality), but provides no explicit text‑bio integration or generative decoder, so it is included as a primary model without a text‑bio bridge or generative component.",Big Bird: Transformers for Longer Sequences,"Transformers-based models, such as BERT, have been one of the most successful deep learning models for NLP. Unfortunately, one of their core limitations is the quadratic dependency (mainly in terms of memory) on the sequence length due to their full attention mechanism. To remedy this, we propose, BigBird, a sparse attention mechanism that reduces this quadratic dependency to linear. We show that BigBird is a universal approximator of sequence functions and is Turing complete, thereby preserving these properties of the quadratic, full attention model. Along the way, our theoretical analysis reveals some of the benefits of having $O(1)$ global tokens (such as CLS), that attend to the entire sequence as part of the sparse attention mechanism. The proposed sparse attention can handle sequences of length up to 8x of what was previously possible using similar hardware. As a consequence of the capability to handle longer context, BigBird drastically improves performance on various NLP tasks such as question answering and summarization. We also propose novel applications to genomics data.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge,,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents BigBird as a new transformer model with NLP improvements (text component) and mentions novel applications to genomics data (bio modality), but provides no explicit description of a text‑bio mapping or bridge. | BigBird is presented as a sparse-attention transformer improving long‑context NLP tasks; the abstract contains no mention of decoder, autoregressive or other generative mechanisms. It highlights broad applicability to many NLP tasks and genomics, indicating foundation‑model‑like breadth.","The abstract presents BigBird as a new transformer model with NLP improvements (text component) and mentions novel applications to genomics data (bio modality), but provides no explicit description of a text‑bio mapping or bridge.","BigBird is presented as a sparse-attention transformer improving long‑context NLP tasks; the abstract contains no mention of decoder, autoregressive or other generative mechanisms. It highlights broad applicability to many NLP tasks and genomics, indicating foundation‑model‑like breadth.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:59:418,BioNexusSentinel: a visual tool for bioregulatory network and cytohistological RNA-seq genetic expression profiling within the context of multicellular simulation research using ChatGPT-augmented software engineering.,"Abstract Summary Motivated by the need to parameterize ongoing multicellular simulation research, this paper documents the culmination of a ChatGPT augmented software engineering cycle resulting in an integrated visual platform for efficient cytohistological RNA-seq and bioregulatory network exploration. As contrasted to other systems and synthetic biology tools, BioNexusSentinel was developed de novo to uniquely combine these features. Reactome served as the primary source of remotely accessible biological models, accessible using BioNexusSentinel’s novel search engine and REST API requests. The innovative, feature-rich gene expression profiler component was developed to enhance the exploratory experience for the researcher, culminating in the cytohistological RNA-seq explorer based on Human Protein Atlas data. A novel cytohistological classifier would be integrated via pre-processed analysis of the RNA-seq data via R statistical language, providing for useful analytical functionality and good performance for the end-user. Implications of the work span prospects for model orthogonality evaluations, gap identification in network modelling, prototyped automatic kinetics parameterization, and downstream simulation and cellular biological state analysis. This unique computational biology software engineering collaboration with generative natural language processing artificial intelligence was shown to enhance worker productivity, with evident benefits in terms of accelerating coding and machine-human intelligence transfer. Availability and implementation BioNexusSentinel project releases, with corresponding data and installation instructions, are available at https://github.com/RichardMatzko/BioNexusSentinel.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,BioNexusSentinel: a visual tool for bioregulatory network and cytohistological RNA-seq genetic expression profiling within the context of multicellular simulation research using ChatGPT-augmented software engineering.,"Abstract Summary Motivated by the need to parameterize ongoing multicellular simulation research, this paper documents the culmination of a ChatGPT augmented software engineering cycle resulting in an integrated visual platform for efficient cytohistological RNA-seq and bioregulatory network exploration. As contrasted to other systems and synthetic biology tools, BioNexusSentinel was developed de novo to uniquely combine these features. Reactome served as the primary source of remotely accessible biological models, accessible using BioNexusSentinel’s novel search engine and REST API requests. The innovative, feature-rich gene expression profiler component was developed to enhance the exploratory experience for the researcher, culminating in the cytohistological RNA-seq explorer based on Human Protein Atlas data. A novel cytohistological classifier would be integrated via pre-processed analysis of the RNA-seq data via R statistical language, providing for useful analytical functionality and good performance for the end-user. Implications of the work span prospects for model orthogonality evaluations, gap identification in network modelling, prototyped automatic kinetics parameterization, and downstream simulation and cellular biological state analysis. This unique computational biology software engineering collaboration with generative natural language processing artificial intelligence was shown to enhance worker productivity, with evident benefits in terms of accelerating coding and machine-human intelligence transfer. Availability and implementation BioNexusSentinel project releases, with corresponding data and installation instructions, are available at https://github.com/R