loratory experience for the researcher, culminating in the cytohistological RNA-seq explorer based on Human Protein Atlas data. A novel cytohistological classifier would be integrated via pre-processed analysis of the RNA-seq data via R statistical language, providing for useful analytical functionality and good performance for the end-user. Implications of the work span prospects for model orthogonality evaluations, gap identification in network modelling, prototyped automatic kinetics parameterization, and downstream simulation and cellular biological state analysis. This unique computational biology software engineering collaboration with generative natural language processing artificial intelligence was shown to enhance worker productivity, with evident benefits in terms of accelerating coding and machine-human intelligence transfer. Availability and implementation BioNexusSentinel project releases, with corresponding data and installation instructions, are available at https://github.com/RichardMatzko/BioNexusSentinel.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a software tool for RNA-seq and bioregulatory network exploration with no natural-language text component or generative model; ChatGPT was used only in development, so it fails EC2 (no text component) and EC3 (not generative).","The paper describes a visual software platform for RNA-seq and network exploration, not a generative text-bio model. It uses ChatGPT only for software engineering assistance, not as a model component. No natural-language text component is present in the tool itself.","The abstract describes a software tool for cytohistological RNA-seq and bioregulatory network exploration, not a generative model. ChatGPT was used only as a coding aid, not as a generative architecture.","The abstract describes a software tool for RNA-seq and bioregulatory network exploration with no natural