./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:60:693,Biologically grounded neocortex computational primitives implemented on neuromorphic hardware improve vision transformer performance,"Significance We implement a biologically grounded cortical circuit motif in neuromorphic hardware and AI architectures to show how experimentally informed neocortical computations, realized through cell-type-specific soft winner-take-all (sWTA) dynamics, can enhance AI. Parvalbumin (PV) provided feedforward inhibition, somatostatin (SST) feedback inhibition, vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) disinhibition, and LAMP5 gain normalization, capturing core mechanisms of cortical gain control and competitive filtering. Hardware implementations demonstrated linear compute-time scaling with load, contrasting the quadratic scaling of simulations. Incorporated as a fixed preprocessing module in Vision Transformers, the motif improved out-of-distribution generalization by ~20% and lowered training cost by enhancing feature selectivity, without requiring additional data or architectural changes. These findings establish a scalable roadmap connecting cortical computation to energy-efficient neuromorphic hardware and robust AI.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Biologically grounded neocortex computational primitives implemented on neuromorphic hardware improve vision transformer performance,"Significance We implement a biologically grounded cortical circuit motif in neuromorphic hardware and AI architectures to show how experimentally informed neocortical computations, realized through cell-type-specific soft winner-take-all (sWTA) dynamics, can enhance AI. Parvalbumin (PV) provided feedforward inhibition, somatostatin (SST) feedback inhibition, vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) disinhibition, and LAMP5 gain normalization, capturing core mechanisms of cortical gain control and competitive filtering. Hardware implementations demonstrated linear compute-time scaling with load, contrasting the quadratic scaling of simulations. Incorporated as a fixed preprocessing module in Vision Transformers, the motif improved out-of-distribution generalization by ~20% and lowered training cost by enhancing feature selectivity, without requiring additional data or architectural changes. These findings establish a scalable roadmap connecting cortical computation to energy-efficient neuromorphic hardware and robust AI.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC1_no_bio_modality,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a biologically grounded preprocessing module for Vision Transformers with no biological data modality, no natural-language text component, and no generative mechanism; thus it is excluded.","The abstract describes neocortex computational primitives for improving vision transformer performance with no mention of text, language, or natural-language components; hence it fails the text component criterion.","The abstract describes a cortical circuit motif used as a fixed preprocessing module for Vision Transformers, with no mention of generative mechanisms or text-bio applications. It does not present a generative text-bio foundational model.","The abstract describes a biologically grounded preprocessing module for Vision Transformers with no biological data modality, no natural-language text component, and no generative mechanism; thus it is excluded.",Biologically grounded neocortex computational primitives implemented on neuromorphic hardware improve vision transformer performance,"Significance We implement a biologically grounded cortical circuit motif in neuromorphic hardware and AI architectures to show how experimentally informed neocortical computations, realized through cell-type-specific soft winner-take-all (sWTA) dynamics, can enhance AI. Parvalbumin (PV) provided feedforward inhibition, somatostatin (SST) feedback inhibition, vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) disinhibition,