ctive intestinal peptide (VIP) disinhibition, and LAMP5 gain normalization, capturing core mechanisms of cortical gain control and competitive filtering. Hardware implementations demonstrated linear compute-time scaling with load, contrasting the quadratic scaling of simulations. Incorporated as a fixed preprocessing module in Vision Transformers, the motif improved out-of-distribution generalization by ~20% and lowered training cost by enhancing feature selectivity, without requiring additional data or architectural changes. These findings establish a scalable roadmap connecting cortical computation to energy-efficient neuromorphic hardware and robust AI.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC1_no_bio_modality,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"Abstract describes a biologically inspired preprocessing module for Vision Transformers; no biological data modality, no text component, no generative model, no text-bio bridge. Excluded.","The abstract describes a biologically inspired cortical circuit motif for vision transformers, with no mention of text, language, or biological data modality relevant to generative text-bio models.",The abstract describes a preprocessing module for Vision Transformers using cortical circuit motifs; no generative text or bio foundation model is presented.,"Abstract describes a biologically inspired preprocessing module for Vision Transformers; no biological data modality, no text component, no generative model, no text-bio bridge. Excluded.",Biologically grounded neocortex computational primitives implemented on neuromorphic hardware improve vision transformer performance,"Significance We implement a biologically grounded cortical circuit motif in neuromorphic hardware and AI architectures to show how experimentally informed neocortical computations, realized through cell-type-specific soft winner-take-all (sWTA) dynamics, can enhance AI. Parvalbumin (PV) provided feedforward inhibition, somatostatin (SST) feedb