./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:66:1192,"Decode-gLM: Tools to Interpret, Audit, and Steer Genomic Language Models","While genomic language models are enabling the  de novo design of entire genomes, they remain challenging to interpret, limiting their trustworthiness. Here, we show that sparse autoencoders (SAEs) trained on Nucleotide Transformer activations decompose hidden representations into interpretable biological features without supervision. Across layers and model sizes, SAEs identified over 100 diverse functional annotations encoded in the model’s activations. This included viral regulatory elements such as the CMV enhancer, despite viral genomes being excluded from training data. Tracing this signal revealed contamination in reference databases, demonstrating that interpretability methods can audit training data and identify hidden data leakage. We then show that Meta-SAEs, trained on the decoder weights of another SAE, can identify conceptual hierarchies encoded in the model, including a more abstract feature related to multiple HIV annotations. We confirmed that the features identified by our SAEs were learned during pretraining through probing a randomly initialised model. Finally, we demonstrate that our SAEs allow us to steer model predictions in biologically meaningful ways, showing that we can use an antibiotic-resistance SAE-feature to steer the model toward the A1408G aminoglycoside-resistance mutation in the ribosomal gene 16S rRNA. Together, these results establish SAEs as a method for both discovery and auditing, providing a toolkit for interpretable and trustworthy genomic foundation models. Readers can explore our findings at  https://interpretglm.netlify.app/ .",exclude_uncertain_unstable,"Decode-gLM: Tools to Interpret, Audit, and Steer Genomic Language Models","While genomic language models are enabling the  de novo design of entire genomes, they remain challenging to interpret, limiting their trustworthiness. Here, we show that sparse autoencoders (SAEs) trained on Nucleotide Transformer activations decompose hidden representations into interpretable biological features without supervision. Across layers and model sizes, SAEs identified over 100 diverse functional annotations encoded in the model’s activations. This included viral regulatory elements such as the CMV enhancer, despite viral genomes being excluded from training data. Tracing this signal revealed contamination in reference databases, demonstrating that interpretability methods can audit training data and identify hidden data leakage. We then show that Meta-SAEs, trained on the decoder weights of another SAE, can identify conceptual hierarchies encoded in the model, including a more abstract feature related to multiple HIV annotations. We confirmed that the features identified by our SAEs were learned during pretraining through probing a randomly initialised model. Finally, we demonstrate that our SAEs allow us to steer model predictions in biologically meaningful ways, showing that we can use an antibiotic-resistance SAE-feature to steer the model toward the A1408G aminoglycoside-resistance mutation in the ribosomal gene 16S rRNA. Together, these results establish SAEs as a method for both discovery and auditing, providing a toolkit for interpretable and trustworthy genomic foundation models. Readers can explore our findings at  https://interpretglm.netlify.app/ .",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes sparse autoencoders for interpreting genomic language models (Nucleotide Transformer) without any natural-language text component, text-bio bridge, or generative mechanism; thus it clearly does not meet inclusion criteria.","The paper describes interpretability tools (SAEs) for genomic language models, not a generative text-bio foundational model. No natural-language text component or text-bio bridge is present.","The paper presents an interpretability toolkit (SAEs) applied to an existing genomic language model (Nucleotide Transformer), not a new generative model. Therefore it is an application wrapper.","The abstract describes sparse autoencoders for interpreting genomic language models (Nucleotide Transformer) without any natural-language text component, text-bio bridge, or generative mechanism; thus it clearly does not meet inclusion criteria.","Decode-gLM: Tools to Interpret, Audit, and Steer Genomic Language Models","While genomic language models are enabling the  de novo design of entire genomes, they remain challenging to interpret, limiting their trustworthiness. Here, we show that sparse autoencoders (SAEs) trained on Nucleotide Transformer activations decompose hidden representations into interpretable biological features without supervision. Across layers and model sizes, SAEs identified over 100 diverse functional annotations encoded in the model’s activations. This included viral regulatory elements such as the CMV enhancer, despite viral genomes being excluded from training data. Tracing this signal revealed contamination in reference databases, demonstrating that interpretability methods can audit training data and identify hidden data leakage. We then show that Meta-SAEs, trained on the decoder weights of another SAE, can identify conceptual hierarchies encoded in the model, including a more abstract feature related to multiple HIV annotations. We confirmed that the features identified by our SAEs were learned during pretraining through probing a randomly initialised model. Finally, we demonstrate that our SAEs allow us to steer model predictions in biologically meaningful ways, showing that we can use an antibiotic-resistance SAE-feature to steer the model toward the A1408G aminoglycoside-resistance mutation in the ribosomal gene 16S rRNA. Together, these results establish SAEs as a method for both discovery and auditing, providing a toolkit for interpretable and trustworthy genomic foundation models. Readers can explore our findings at  https://interpretglm.netlify.app/ .",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes tools for interpreting genomic language models using sparse autoencoders on Nucleotide Transformer activations, with no natural-language text component or generative model; thus it is excluded as an application wrapper with no text component and no generative model.","The abstract describes tools for interpreting genomic language models (biological sequences) with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or text-bio alignment, so it lacks a substantive text component.","The paper focuses on interpretability tools (sparse autoencoders) applied to existing genomic language models, not on proposing a new generative model. The candidate contribution is not generative.","The abstract describes tools for interpreting genomic language models using sparse autoencoders on Nucleotide Transformer activations, with no natural-language text component or generative model; thus it is excluded as an application wrapper with no text component and no generative model.","Decode-gLM: Tools to Interpret, Audit, and Steer Genomic Language Models","While genomic language models are enabling the  de novo design of entire genomes, they remain challenging to interpret, limiting their trustworthiness. Here, we show that sparse autoencoders (SAEs) trained on Nucleotide Transformer activations decompose hidden representations into interpretable biological features without supervision. Across layers and model sizes, SAEs identified over 100 diverse functional annotations encoded in the model’s activations. This included viral regulatory elements such as the CMV enhancer, despite viral genomes being excluded from training data. Tracing this signal revealed contamination in reference databases, demonstrating that interpretability methods can audit training data and identify hidden data leakage. We then show that Meta-SAEs, trained on the decoder weights of another SAE, can identify conceptual hierarchies encoded in the model, including a more abstract feature related to multiple HIV annotations. We confirmed that the features identified by our SAEs were learned during pretraining through probing a randomly initialised model. Finally, we demonstrate that our SAEs allow us to steer model predictions in biologically meaningful ways, showing that we can use an antibiotic-resistance SAE-feature to steer the model toward the A1408G aminoglycoside-resistance mutation in the ribosomal gene 16S rRNA. Together, these results establish SAEs as a method for both discovery and auditing, providing a toolkit for interpretable and trustworthy genomic foundation models. Readers can explore our findings at  https://interpretglm.netlify.app/ .",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The paper describes tools (SAEs) for interpreting and steering existing genomic language models (Nucleotide Transformer), not a new generative text-bio model; no natural-language text component is present, and the work is an application wrapper around an existing model. | The paper describes tools (SAEs) to interpret and steer existing genomic language models like Nucleotide Transformer, not a new generative model. It is an application wrapper around an existing model.","The paper describes tools (SAEs) for interpreting and steering existing genomic language models (Nucleotide Transformer), not a new generative text-bio model; no natural-language text component is present, and the work is an application wrapper around an existing model.","The paper describes tools (SAEs) to interpret and steer existing genomic language models like Nucleotide Transformer, not a new generative model. It is an application wrapper around an existing model.",,"Decod