./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:145: This study demonstrates that multi-modal MRIs can successfully identify glioblastoma subtypes characterized by deep learning derived radiomic features. Future work will focus on validation with comprehensive molecular and tissue-based profiles, survival outcomes, and treatment responses to support personalized medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract focuses on MRI-based deep learning feature extraction and clustering with no natural-language text or generative component, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The work applies a pretrained MAE (a self‑supervised generative model) for radiomic feature extraction and clustering without introducing a new model, fitting the application wrapper category; the MAE’s large pretraining dataset provides foundation‑model evidence.","The abstract focuses on MRI-based deep learning feature extraction and clustering with no natural-language text or generative component, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","The work applies a pretrained MAE (a self‑supervised generative model) for radiomic feature extraction and clustering without introducing a new model, fitting the application wrapper category; the MAE’s large pretraining dataset provides foundation‑model evidence.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:146:980,Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes integrative analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of natural-language text, text-bio alignment, or generative modeling; thus it fails text component and generative criteria.","No text/language component; the study uses machine learning on clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for subtyping, without any natural-language text or text-bio alignment.","Abstract describes PD subtyping using ML/DL and drug repurposing; no generative model, text generation, or foundation model evidence present.","The abstract describes integrative analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of natural-language text, text-bio alignment, or generative modeling; thus it fails text component and generative criteria.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a machine learning analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping, with no mention of natural language text or generative modeling. Excluded because no text component and no generative model.","The paper applies machine learning and deep learning to clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for Parkinson's subtyping and drug repurposing; no generative model, natural-language text component, or text-bio bridge is described, so it is out of scope.","The abstract describes subtyping and progression analysis of Parkinson's disease using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of generative mechanisms, text generation, or biological sequence generation.","The abstract describes a machine learning analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping, with no mention of natural language text or generative modeling. Excluded because no text component and no generative model.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world pat