ract describes a Random Forest‑based multimodal prediction of distant metastasis with no mention of generation, decoding, or generative mechanisms.","The abstract describes gene expression, clinical data, and histopathology images but provides no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so the text component and bridge are absent.","The abstract describes a Random Forest‑based multimodal prediction of distant metastasis with no mention of generation, decoding, or generative mechanisms.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:158:1129,Multimodal FACED imaging for large-scale single-cell morphological profiling,"Free-space angular-chirp-enhanced delay (FACED) is an ultrafast laser-scanning technique that allows for high imaging speed at the scale orders of magnitude greater than the current technologies. However, this speed advantage has only been restricted to bright-field and fluorescence imaging—limiting the variety of image contents and hindering its applicability in image-based bioassay, which increasingly demands rich phenotypic readout at a large scale. Here, we present a new high-speed quantitative phase imaging (QPI) based on time-interleaved phase-gradient FACED image detection. We further integrate this system with a microfluidic flow cytometer platform that enables synchronized and co-registered single-cell QPI and fluorescence imaging at an imaging throughput of 77 000 cells/s with sub-cellular resolution. Combined with deep learning, this platform empowers comprehensive image-based profiling of single-cell biophysical phenotypes that can offer not only sufficient label-free power for cell-type classification but also cell-cycle phase tracking with high accuracy comparable to the gold-standard fluorescence method. This platform further enables correlative, compartment-specific single-cell analysis of the spatially resolved biophysical profiles at the throughput inaccessible with existing QPI methods. The high imaging throughput and content given by this multimodal FACED imaging system could open new opportunities in image-based single-cell analysis, especially systematic analysis that correlates the biophysical and biochemical information of cells, and provide new mechanistic insights into biophysical heterogeneities in many biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Multimodal FACED imaging for large-scale single-cell morphological profiling,"Free-space angular-chirp-enhanced delay (FACED) is an ultrafast laser-scanning technique that allows for high imaging speed at the scale orders of magnitude greater than the current technologies. However, this speed advantage has only been restricted to bright-field and fluorescence imaging—limiting the variety of image contents and hindering its applicability in image-based bioassay, which increasingly demands rich phenotypic readout at a large scale. Here, we present a new high-speed quantitative phase imaging (QPI) based on time-interleaved phase-gradient FACED image detection. We further integrate this system with a microfluidic flow cytometer platform that enables synchronized and co-registered single-cell QPI and fluorescence imaging at an imaging throughput of 77 000 cells/s with sub-cellular resolution. Combined with deep learning, this platform empowers comprehensive image-based profiling of single-cell biophysical phenotypes that can offer not only sufficient label-free power for cell-type classification but also cell-cycle phase tracking with high accuracy comparable to the gold-standard fluorescence method. This platform further enables correlative, compartment-specific single-cell analysis of the spatially resolved biophysical profiles at the throughput inaccessible with existing QPI methods. The high imaging throughput and content given by this multimodal FACED imaging system could open new opportunities in image-based single-cell analysis, especially systematic analysis that correlates the biophysical and biochemical information of cells, and provide new mechanistic insights into biophysical heterogeneities in many biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The abstract describes a multimodal imaging system for single-cell morphological profiling with deep learning, but no text/language component or text-bio bridge is mentioned. | The paper describes an imaging system and deep learning for cell-type classification and cell-cycle tracking, not a generative model; no evidence of generative architecture or foundation-model characteristics.","The abstract describes a multimodal imaging system for single-cell morphological profiling with deep learning, but no text/language component or text-bio bridge is mentioned.","The paper describes an imaging system and deep learning for cell-type classification and cell-cycle tracking, not a generative model; no evidence of generative architecture or foundation-model characteristics.",,Multimodal FACED imaging for large-scale single-cell morphological profiling,"Free-space angular-chirp-enhanced delay (FACED) is an ultrafast laser-scanning technique that allows for high imaging speed at the scale orders of magnitude greater than the current technologies. However, this speed advantage has only been restricted to bright-field and fluorescence imaging—limiting the variety of image contents and hindering its applicability in image-based bioassay, which increasingly demands rich phenotypic readout at a large scale. Here, we present a new high-speed quantitative phase imaging (QPI) based on time-interleaved phase-gradient FACED image detection. We further integrate this system with a microfluidic flow cytometer platform that enables synchronized and co-registered single-cell QPI and fluorescence imaging at an imaging throughput of 77 000 cells/s with sub-cellular resolution. Combined with deep learning, this platform empowers comprehensive image-based profiling of single-cell biophysical phenotypes that can offer not only sufficient label-free power for cell-type classification but also cell-cycle phase tracking with high accuracy comparable to the gold-standard fluorescence method. This platform further enables correlative, compartment-specific single-cell analysis of the spatially resolved biophysical profiles at the throughput inaccessible with existing QPI methods. The high imaging throughput and content given by this multimodal FACED imaging system could open new opportunities in image-based single-cell analysis, especially systematic analysis that correlates the biophysical and biochemical information of cells, and provide new mechanistic insights into biophysical heterogeneities in many biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes an imaging system and deep learning classification without any text component or generative modeling, so it is excluded.","The paper describes a multimodal imaging system and deep learning for single-cell morphological profiling, with no mention of text, language, or natural-language components; thus it clearly lacks a text component.","The abstract describes a multimodal imaging system using deep learning for cell-type classification and cell-cycle tracking, not a generative model; no text generation or foundation model characteristics are mentioned.","The abstract describes an imaging system and deep learning classification without any text component or generative modeling, so it is excluded.",Multimodal FACED imaging for large-scale single-cell morphological profiling,"Free-space angular-chirp-enhanced delay (FACED) is an ultrafast laser-scanning technique that allows for high imaging speed at the scale orders of magnitude greater than the current technologies. However, this speed advantage has only been restricted to bright-field and fluorescence imaging—limiting the variety of image contents and hindering its applicability in image-based bioassay, which increasingly demands rich phenotypic readout at a large scale. Here, we present a new high-speed quantitative phase imaging (QPI) based on time-interleaved phase-gradient FACED image detection. We further integrate this system with a microfluidic flow cytometer platform that enables synchronized and co-registered single-cell QPI and fluorescence imaging at an imaging throughput of 77 000 cells/s with sub-cellular resolution. Combined with deep learning, this platform empowers comprehensive image-based profiling of single-cell biophysical phenotypes that can offer not only sufficient label-free power for cell-type classification but also cell-cycle phase tracking with high accuracy comparable to the gold-standard fluorescence method. This platform further enables correlative, compartment-specific single-cell analysis of the spatially resolved biophysical profiles at the throughput inaccessible with existing QPI methods. The high imaging throughput and content given by this multimodal FACED imaging system could open new opportunities in image-based single-cell analysis, especially systematic analysis that correlates the biophysical and biochemical information of cells, and provide new mechanistic insights into biophysical heterogeneities in many biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"Abstract describes a new imaging system and deep learning classifier for single-cell profiling; no natural-language text or generative model is present, so it fails text component and generative criteria.","The abstract describes an imaging-based deep learning platform for single-cell profiling with no mention of text, language, or natural-language components; thus it does not meet the text component criterion.","The abstract describes an imaging system and deep learning for classification and tracking, with no mention of generative models, text generation, or foundation model characteristics.","Abstract describes a new imaging system and deep learning classifier for single-cell profiling; no natural-language text or generative model is present, so it fails text component and generative criteria.",Multimodal FACED imaging for large-scale single-cell morphological profiling,"Free-space angular-chirp-enhanced delay (FACED) is an ultrafast laser-scanning technique that allows for high imaging speed at the scale orders of magnitude greater than the current technologies. However, this speed advantage has only been restricted to bright-field and fluorescence imaging—limiting the variety of image contents and hindering its applicability in image-based bioassay, which increasingly demands rich phenotypic readout at a large scale. Here, we present a new high-speed quantitative phase imaging (QPI) based on time-interleaved phase-gradient FACED image detection. We further integrate this system with a microfluidic flow cytometer platform that enables synchronized and co-registered single-cell QPI and fluorescence imaging at an imaging throughput of 77 000 cells/s with sub-cellular resolution. Combined with deep learning, this platform empowers comprehensive image-based profiling of single-cell biophysical phenotypes that can offer not only sufficient label-free power for cell-type classification but also cell-cycle phase tracking with high accuracy comparable to the gold-standard fluorescence method. This platform further enables correlative, compartment-specific single-cell analysis of the spatially resolved biophysical profiles at the throughput inaccessible with existing QPI methods. The high imaging throughput and content given by this multimodal FACED imaging system could open new opportunities in image-based single-cell analysis, especially systematic analysis that correlates the biophysical and biochemical information of cells, and provide new mechanistic insights into biophysical heterogeneities in many biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a high‑speed multimodal imaging system with deep‑learning classification but contains no natural‑language text component, text‑bio alignment, or generative mechanism, thus it is excluded under EC3_not_generative.","The abstract describes a new high‑speed quantitative phase imaging system and deep‑learning analysis for single‑cell profiling, with no mention of natural‑language text or text‑based conditioning.","The abstract presents a high‑speed multimodal imaging platform with deep‑learning analysis for cell‑type classification and phase tracking, but it does not describe any generative architecture (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion).","The abstract describes a high‑speed multimodal imaging system with deep‑learning classification but contains no natural‑language text component, text‑bio alignment, or generative mechanism, thus it is excluded under EC3_not_generative.",Multimodal FACED imaging for large-scale single-cell morphological profiling,"Free-space angular-chirp-enhanced delay (FACED) is an ultrafast laser-scanning technique that allows for high imaging speed at the scale orders of magnitude greater than the current technologies. However, this speed advantage has only been restricted to bright-field and fluorescence imaging—limiting the variety of image contents and hindering its applicability in image-based bioassay, which increasingly demands rich phenotypic readout at a large scale. Here, we present a new high-speed quantitative phase imaging (QPI) based on time-interleaved phase-gradient FACED image detection. We further integrate this system with a microfluidic flow cytometer platform that enables synchronized and co-registered single-cell QPI and fluorescence imaging at an imaging throughput of 77 000 cells/s with sub-cellular resolution. Combined with deep learning, this platform empowers comprehensive image-based profiling of single-cell biophysical phenotypes that can offer not only sufficient label-free power for cell-type classification but also cell-cycle phase tracking with high accuracy comparable to the gold-standard fluorescence method. This platform further enables correlative, compartment-specific single-cell analysis of the spatially resolved biophysical profiles at the throughput inaccessible with existing QPI methods. The high imaging throughput and content given by this multimodal FACED imaging system could open new opportunities in image-based single-cell analysis, especially systematic analysis that correlates the biophysical and biochemical information of cells, and provide new mechanistic insights into biophysical heterogeneities in many biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a high‑speed imaging system and deep‑learning‑based profiling of single‑cell morphology, with no mention of natural‑language text, text‑bio alignment, or generative modeling. Therefore it lacks the required text component and bridge, leading to exclusion (EC2_no_text_component).","The abstract details a high-speed imaging platform and deep‑learning analysis of single‑cell images, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or captions, indicating absence of a text component and bridge.","The abstract focuses on a high‑speed imaging technique and deep‑learning‑based profiling, with no indication of a generative architecture or foundation‑model pretraining.","The abstract describes a high‑speed imaging system and deep‑learning‑based profiling of single‑cell morphology, with no mention of natural‑language text, text‑bio alignment, or generative modeling. Therefore it lacks the required text component and bridge, leading to exclusion (EC2_no_text_component)."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:159:612,Natural language processing and recurrent network models for identifying genomic mutation-associated cancer treatment change from patient progress notes.,"Abstract Objectives Natural language processing (NLP) and machine learning approaches were used to build classifiers to identify genomic-related treatment changes in the free-text visit progress notes of cancer patients. Methods We obtained 5889 deidentified progress reports (2439 words on average) for 755 cancer patients who have undergone a clinical next generation sequencing (NGS) testing in Wake Forest Baptist Comprehensive Cancer Center for our data analyses. An NLP system was implemented to process the free-text data and extract NGS-related information. Three types of recurrent neural network (RNN) namely, gated recurrent unit, long short-term memory (LSTM), and bidirectional LSTM (LSTM_Bi) were applied to classify documents to the treatment-change and no-treatment-change groups. Further, we compared the performances of RNNs to 5 machine learning algorithms including Naive Bayes, K-nearest Neighbor, Support Vector Machine for classification, Random forest, and Logistic Regression. Results Our results suggested that, overall, RNNs outperformed traditional machine learning algorithms, and LSTM_Bi showed the best performance among the RNNs in terms of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score. In addition, pretrained word embedding can improve the accuracy of LSTM by 3.4% and reduce the training time by more than 60%. Discussion and Conclusion NLP and RNN-based text mining solutions have demonstrated advantages in information retrieval and document classification tasks for unstructured clinical progress notes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Natural language processing and recurrent network models for identifying genomic mutation-associated cancer treatment change from patient progress notes.,"Abstract Objectives Natural language processing (NLP) and machine learning approaches were used to build classifiers to identify genomic-related treatment changes in the free-text visit progress notes of cancer patients. Methods We obtained 5889 deidentified progress reports (2439 words on average) for 755 cancer patients who have undergone a clinical next generation sequencing (NGS) testing in Wake Forest Baptist Comprehensive Cancer Center for our data analyses. An NLP system was implemented to process the free-text data and extract NGS-related information. Three types of recurrent neural network (RNN) namely, gated recurrent unit, long short-term memory (LSTM), and bidirectional LSTM (LSTM_Bi) were applied to classify documents to the treatment-change and no-treatment-change groups. Further, we compared the performances of RNNs to 5 machine learning algorithms including Naive Bayes, K-nearest Neighbor, Support Vector Machine for classification, Random forest, and Logistic Regression. Results Our results suggested that, overall, RNNs outperformed traditional machine learning algorithms, and LSTM_Bi showed the best performance among the RNNs in terms of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score. In addition, pretrained word embedding can improve the accuracy of LSTM by 3.4% and reduce the training time by more than 60%. Discussion and Conclusion NLP and RNN-based text mining solutions have demonstrated advantages in information retrieval and document classification tasks for unstructured clinical progress notes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,free-text visit progress notes,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a classification task using RNNs on clinical progress notes, not a generative model. No text-bio generation or alignment, so excluded as not generative.","Paper presents classifiers for classifying clinical progress notes, not a generative text-bio foundational model. No evidence of text-bio generation or alignment.","The abstract describes RNN-based classifiers for identifying treatment changes from clinical notes, a classification task with no generative mechanism such as autoregressive, decoder, or diffusion.","The abstract describes a classification task using RNNs on clinical progress notes, not a generative model. No text-bio generation or alignment, so excluded as not generative.",Natural language processing and recurrent network models for identifying genomic mutation-associated cancer treatment change from patient progress notes.,"Abstract Objectives Natural language processing (NLP) and machine learning approaches were used to build classifiers to identify genomic-related treatment changes in the free-text visit progress notes of cancer patients. Methods We obtained 5889 deidentified progress reports (2439 words on average) for 755 cancer patients who have undergone a clinical next generation sequencing (NGS) testing in Wake Forest Baptist Comprehensive Cancer Center for our data analyses. An NLP system was implemented to process the free-text data and extract NGS-related information. Three types of recurrent neural network (RNN) namely, gated recurrent unit, long short-term memory (LSTM), and bidirectional LSTM (LSTM_Bi) were applied to classify documents to the treatment-change and no-treatment-change groups. Further, we compared the performances of RNNs to 5 machine learning algorithms including Naive Bayes, K-nearest Neighbor, Support Vector Machine for classification, Random forest, and Logistic Regression. Results Our results suggested that, overall, RNNs outperformed traditional machine learning algorithms, and LSTM_Bi showed the best performance among the RNNs in terms of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score. In addition, pretrained word embedding can improve the accuracy of LSTM by 3.4% and reduce the training time by more than 60%. Discussion and Conclusion NLP and RNN-based text mining solutions have demonstrated advantages in information retrieval and document classification tasks for unstructured clinical progress notes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,free-text visit progress notes,none,none,clear_exclude,"The paper applies NLP and RNN classifiers to clinical progress notes for classification of treatment changes; it does not present a generative model or a text-bio bridge, and is an application wrapper.","The paper applies NLP and RNN classifiers to patient progress notes for a classification task; it does not present a generative text-bio foundational model, making it an application wrapper.","The paper builds RNN classifiers for document classification (treatment change vs no treatment change) with no generative mechanism; it is a predictive classification system, not a generative model.","The paper applies NLP and RNN classifiers to clinical progress notes for classification of treatment changes; it does not present a generative model or a text-bio bridge, and is an application wryes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a self-supervised foundation model trained on thousands of tumors and used on unseen H&E images across multiple cancer types. It does not explicitly state a generative mechanism such as a decoder, autoregressive model, or diffusion model.","evidence_snippet":"“A self-supervised AI foundation model was trained ... using purpose-built multimodal training data from thousands of human tumors”"},{"record_id":"rec_000108","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a scoping review of 20 studies, not a primary model report. The mentions of LLM and foundation models are survey content rather than evidence for a candidate generative model.","evidence_snippet":"“This scoping review systematically maps AI- and DL-based methodologies”"},{"record_id":"rec_000109","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly describes a “conditional variational autoencoder framework,” which is a genuinely generative architecture. It presents a task-specific virtual cell model, but not clear foundation-model-like broad pretraining or applicability.","evidence_snippet":"“a lightweight conditional variational autoencoder framework”"},{"record_id":"rec_000110","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The model is described as a “modernized BERT-style back-bone,” which is encoder-style rather than clearly generative. The abstract does give foundation-model evidence through genomic sequence modeling and benchmark performance.","evidence_snippet":"“modernized BERT-style back-bone ... adapted to genomic sequence modeling”"},{"record_id":"rec_000111","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract states that the authors performed a “systematic review” of AI-based prognostic models. It summarizes existing recurrence and RFS prediction studies rather than describing a new generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“We performed a contemporary systematic review”"},{"record_id":"rec_000112","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract applies pre-trained immune system models and a peripheral immune system encoder to predict response and toxicity. This is an application of existing foundation-like models, not a new generative model.","evidence_snippet":"“pre-trained immunotype models” and “a peripheral immune system encoder trained on the BostonGene patient database”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0154.stderr.log:3158:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0045.stderr.log:1220:data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0030.stderr.log:135:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000233","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a machine-learning profiling framework for spatial ecotypes, not a generative architecture. No foundation-model-style pretraining or transfer evidence is stated.","evidence_snippet":"“machine-learning framework for multi-analyte profiling” and “identified nine SEs”"},{"record_id":"rec_000234","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The framework uses a large language model-based reasoner to interpret multimodal outputs, so a generative component is present. However, the abstract does not show foundation-model-like pretraining or broad transferable representation learning for the candidate system.","evidence_snippet":"“a large language model-based reasoner” and “chain-of-thought reasoning”"},{"record_id":"rec_000235","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly calls MAMMAL a foundation model and says it was pre-trained on 2 billion samples. It also states that it supports generative tasks on cross-modal inputs.","evidence_snippet":"“a foundation model for cross-modal learning,” “pre-trained on 2 billion samples,” and “supports ... generative tasks”"},{"record_id":"rec_000236","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a literature review of deep learning and explainable AI for ovarian cancer detection. It does not describe a candidate generative model or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“A Comprehensive Literature Review” and “This review synthesizes recent advances”"},{"record_id":"rec_000237","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"DeepSeMS is described as a transformer-based large language model that predicts chemical structures from gene clusters, which is generative. The abstract does not provide clear evidence of broad pretraining or other foundation-model-like characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"“transformer-based large language model” that “predicts secondary metabolite chemical structures”"},{"record_id":"rec_000238","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a protocol for using Geneformer rather than a new model paper. The abstract explicitly states Geneformer is pretrained on a large-scale corpus, but the described use is predictive and perturbative rather than generative.","evidence_snippet":"“Geneformer is a foundational artificial intelligence model” and “pretrained on a large-scale corpus of single-cell transcriptomes”"},{"record_id":"rec_000239","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract clearly describes a comprehensive review of RNA-binding proteins in neuroinflammation. It does not present a model, let alone a generative or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“This review systematically organizes” and “The review aims to construct a comprehensive theoretical framework”"},{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper studies LLM-based generation of visualization grammars, so a generative workflow is present. But it is an agentic authoring/application paper, and the abstract does not establish foundation-model-style pretraining for a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“vanilla LLM generation” and “All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0154.stderr.log:3159:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0045.stderr.log:1221:data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0035.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000273","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract reads like a broad overview of AI methods in genomics, mentioning example uses such as SVMs and language models rather than a single proposed model. No clear generative architecture or foundation-model pretraining is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"In genomics, SVMs have been applied...\" and \"Language model paradigms extend naturally to RNA\""},{"record_id":"rec_000274","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract clearly describes a pre-trained model on 30,000+ tumors with generalization across cancer types, which is foundation-model-like evidence. It does not explicitly state a decoder, autoregressive, or other clearly generative mechanism, so generative status is unclear.","evidence_snippet":"\"pre-trained from a large corpus of genomic alterations across 30,000+ tumors\" and \"accurately reconstructs held-out mutations\""},{"record_id":"rec_000275","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a benchmarking study of algorithms for perturbation response prediction, not a primary generative model paper. The abstract only references comparing predictive performance and generalizability.","evidence_snippet":"\"Benchmarking algorithms for generalizable single-cell perturbation response prediction\""},{"record_id":"rec_000276","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract fragment indicate a methods overview on analyzing single-cell and genomic data, with no distinct proposed generative model. There is no foundation-model evidence in the provided text.","evidence_snippet":"\"Statistical and Artificial Intelligence Methods for Analyzing Single-Cell and Genomic Data\""},{"record_id":"rec_000277","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work is a literature-mining and modeling framework that weights gene regulations and builds an ODE model, using an LLM-based framework as a tool. It is not presented as a generative text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"we developed a framework to assign quantitative, context-dependent weights\" and \"The large language model-based framework allows...\""},{"record_id":"rec_000278","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly names retrieval-augmented generation, so a generative mechanism is present. The abstract fragment provides no evidence of broad pretraining or other foundation-model characteristics for the candidate system.","evidence_snippet":"\"Enhancing cell type annotation... using retrieval-augmented generation\""},{"record_id":"rec_000279","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly references generative adversarial networks, which is clear generative-model evidence. The provided abstract text mentions foundation model priors, but not enough to establish that the candidate model itself has foundation-model-like pretraining or breadth.","evidence_snippet":"\"Generative Adversarial Networks\" and \"augmented with foundation model priors\""},{"record_id":"rec_000280","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title is a broad position/review-style statement on interpretable multimodal biomedical AI, and the abstract fragment cites Evo as an example rather than proposing a new model. No clear generative foundation model is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"TOWARD INTERPRETABLE MULTI-OMICS MULTIMODAL BIOMEDICAL ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE\" and \"Using Evo, a 7-billion-parameter language model\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0154.stderr.log:3160:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0045.stderr.log:1222:data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0032.stderr.log:135:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes parameter-efficient fine-tuning of existing scLLMs rather than a new generative model. It mentions LLMs, but the candidate here is a tuning framework, not a foundation model itself.","evidence_snippet":"“parameter-efficient fine-tuning ... integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs”"},{"record_id":"rec_000250","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract identify scpFormer as a transformer-based foundation model and say it is pre-trained. The snippet does not clearly state a generative mechanism such as decoding, autoregression, or diffusion.","evidence_snippet":"“a transformer-based foundation model” and “Pre-trained on”"},{"record_id":"rec_000251","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly includes a residual variational autoencoder, which is a generative architecture. It also uses a pre-trained foundation model, but the work is an application pipeline for phenotype-associated subpopulation identification.","evidence_snippet":"“combines a pre-trained foundation model (PFM) with a residual variational autoencoder (Res-VAE)”"},{"record_id":"rec_000252","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly states probabilistic generation of expression profiles and virtual cell synthesis. It also presents xVERSE as a foundation model with broad representation learning and cross-modality utility.","evidence_snippet":"“coupling batch-invariant representation learning with the probabilistic generation of expression profiles”"},{"record_id":"rec_000253","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title says the model is a foundation model and the abstract mentions it is pre-trained on a massive collection of gene sets. The provided text does not clearly describe a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“gene set foundation model pre-trained on a massive collection of diverse gene sets”"},{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title presents ChromFound as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The abstract snippet does not give enough mechanism detail to confirm a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data”"},{"record_id":"rec_000255","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly includes latent diffusion, which is a generative mechanism. It also frames the method as a foundation model representation approach for spatial gene expression prediction.","evidence_snippet":"“Latent diffusion Alignment via Graph” and “Foundation model representation”"},{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a benchmarking study over 79 LLMs and a deployed platform for cell type annotation. It does not present a new generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models” and “developed AICellType ... a ... platform”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0154.stderr.log:3161:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0045.stderr.log:1223:data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0001.response.txt:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000001","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a mini-review, not a primary model paper. It discusses self-supervised vision foundation models and LLMs as literature examples, but does not present a candidate generative model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This mini-review critically evaluates...\" and mentions \"self-supervised Vision Foundation Models\" and \"large language models\" in the review context"},{"record_id":"rec_000002","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review of AI assistants and translational limitations, not a primary model description. The abstract discusses foundation models and LLMs as tools in general rather than a specific candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review evaluates the current applications of AI in neurodegenerative disease research\" and mentions \"foundation models and large language models\""},{"record_id":"rec_000003","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly describes a \"cycle-consistent deep generative model\" for multimodal integration. It does not provide evidence of broad pretraining or foundation-model-like scope; it is a task-specific integration model.","evidence_snippet":"\"a spatially-regularized, cycle-consistent deep generative model\" and \"learns a shared latent representation\""},{"record_id":"rec_000004","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"OTTER uses modality-specific variational autoencoders, which is generative modeling. The abstract frames it as a disease-specific multi-omics integration framework and does not show foundation-model-like broad pretraining or general-purpose applicability.","evidence_snippet":"\"modality-specific variational autoencoder\" and \"The framework is generalizable to other cancers and multi-omics integration tasks\""},{"record_id":"rec_000005","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a predictive pathogenicity model built with a transformer encoder and CNNs, not a genuinely generative architecture. Pretrained DNA language model features are used as inputs, but the candidate model itself is a downstream predictor.","evidence_snippet":"\"a deep learning framework designed for precise assessment\" and \"By employing a transformer encoder\"; \"derived from a pretrained DNA language model\""},{"record_id":"rec_000006","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review article on multimodal AI for occult lymph node metastasis, not a primary model paper. It summarizes deep learning and fusion approaches rather than presenting one candidate generative model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Purpose of Review\" and \"This review examines\""},{"record_id":"rec_000007","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"THC-Net is presented as a prediction model for chromatin compartments, with no generative mechanism described. The abstract reports cross-cell-type predictive performance rather than foundation-model-like pretraining or broad transferable representation learning.","evidence_snippet":"\"an attention-based deep learning model for chromatin compartment prediction\" and \"predict genomic A/B compartments\""},{"record_id":"rec_000008","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this is a review article and that no novel software is distributed. It surveys generative and foundation model strategies rather than presenting a candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This is a review article; no novel software is distributed\" and \"generative and foundation model strategies\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0154.stderr.log:3162:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0045.stderr.log:1228:data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0019.stderr.log:135:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000145","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a systematic evaluation of 11 genomic language models and critiques their pretraining paradigm, rather than presenting a new model. It does not clearly specify a generative architecture or broader foundation-model properties.","evidence_snippet":"“systematically evaluated 11 representative gLMs” and “canonical sequence-only self-supervised pretraining paradigm”"},{"record_id":"rec_000146","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Gemini Pro is used zero-shot as an existing LLM to make ranked primary-site predictions, so this is an application wrapper rather than a new model paper. The abstract explicitly identifies it as a large language model used without fine-tuning.","evidence_snippet":"“Large Language Models (LLMs), such as Gemini Pro” and “zero-shot prompt”"},{"record_id":"rec_000147","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a broad review of AI in drug discovery, not a primary generative model paper. It mentions generative models in passing, but the abstract does not describe a specific generative architecture or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“This review provides a comprehensive analysis” and “generative models such as GANs and reinforcement learning”"},{"record_id":"rec_000148","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents SciCore-Omics as a tri-modal foundation model trained on a large paired image-gene-text dataset. It also reports zero-shot generalization and language-grounded outputs, supporting both generative and foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"“the first tri-modal foundation model linking histology images, spatial transcriptomics, and biological language” and “robust zero-shot generalization”"},{"record_id":"rec_000149","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a review of AI-driven ASD diagnosis across modalities and methods. It does not describe a new generative architecture or provide foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"“This review provides a comprehensive analysis” and “deep learning, traditional machine learning, and multimodal fusion techniques”"},{"record_id":"rec_000150","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"GRNPred is an encoder-based graph transformer with masked gene-expression reconstruction and supervised edge prediction, which is predictive rather than clearly generative. The abstract does describe self-supervised pretraining on unlabeled data, but not a foundation model with broad applicability.","evidence_snippet":"“graph transformer encoder” and “masked gene-expression reconstruction”"},{"record_id":"rec_000151","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes multimodal chemical-screening analysis frameworks for decoding perturbation effects, not a generative model. It is a methods paper about screening and inference, with no foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"“we develop MoCAVI, a contrastive analysis framework” and “PERCISTRA, a pipeline that infers causal links”"},{"record_id":"rec_000152","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"H2O is presented as a generalist foundation model trained on a very large pan-tissue paired dataset and used to infer spatial transcriptomics and proteomics from histology. The abstract explicitly emphasizes broad generalizability and computational generation of omics landscapes.","evidence_snippet":"“a foundation model bridging histopathology to spatial multi-omics profiling” and “trained on a pan-tissue dataset of 1.3 million paired H&E-spatial patches”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0154.stderr.log:3163:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0045.stderr.log:1229:data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0010.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000073","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a predictive Transformer framework for perturbation-response prediction, not a generative architecture. It uses foundation-model embeddings as inputs, but does not show the candidate model is a foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"predict single-cell transcriptomic responses\" and \"integrates large language model embeddings\""},{"record_id":"rec_000074","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly names a variational autoencoder, which is a generative model. It presents a task-specific clustering framework rather than a broadly pretrained foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"scalable variational autoencoder framework\""},{"record_id":"rec_000075","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"SpaMode is described as an integration and disentanglement framework for spatial multi-omics, not a generative text-bio model. The abstract gives no evidence of broad pretraining or foundation-model behavior.","evidence_snippet":"\"framework designed to accommodate spatial multi-omic integration\""},{"record_id":"rec_000076","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a federated predictive diagnosis framework using CNNs, LSTMs, and attention for CKD risk assessment. The abstract does not describe any generative mechanism or foundation-model-like pretraining.","evidence_snippet":"\"single predictive engine\" with \"CNNs\" and \"LSTM networks\""},{"record_id":"rec_000077","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The core system is a multi-task classifier/regressor for phenotyping and protein-expression prediction. Although an LLM is used to generate summaries, the abstract does not describe the main architecture as genuinely generative.","evidence_snippet":"\"jointly performs WBC classification and continuous protein-expression regression\" and \"LLM that generates concise summaries\""},{"record_id":"rec_000078","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This paper introduces BGCs-Bench and evaluates long-context genome language models on downstream tasks. It is a benchmark/evaluation resource rather than a new generative model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"We introduce BGCs-Bench, a unified benchmark\""},{"record_id":"rec_000079","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract states that this is a review of spatial transcriptomics and related technologies. It does not present a candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review discusses the rapidly evolving landscape of spatial technologies\""},{"record_id":"rec_000080","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"GATHER is a retrieval method for zero-shot cell-type annotation, with evidence selection and LLM calls for reasoning. The abstract does not describe a generative architecture or foundation-model-like pretraining.","evidence_snippet":"\"convergence-centric retriever\" and \"without LLM involvement during retrieval\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0154.stderr.log:3164:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0045.stderr.log:1230:data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0015.response.txt:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000113","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a causal multimodal predictive model for chemotherapy benefit, not a generative architecture. It uses a pathology foundation model as an encoder, but the proposed ATX model itself is not foundation-model-like.","evidence_snippet":"\"predicts the patient-specific benefit\"; \"WSIs were encoded via a pathology foundation model and integrated with clinical variables\""},{"record_id":"rec_000114","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an intervention-aware representation-learning/distillation framework, not a generative model. The foundation-model mention refers to the teacher's single-cell encoder, not a new foundation model in the paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"intervention-aware distillation framework\"; \"an image-only student learns to predict these distributions\"; \"fine-tuned single-cell foundation model\""},{"record_id":"rec_000115","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract calls it a generative AI-driven pipeline and says it leverages large language models, but it does not clearly describe a new generative architecture. It reads as a pipeline built around existing tools for PTM detection and structural modeling.","evidence_snippet":"\"generative AI-driven pipeline\"; \"PTMdiscoverer leverages large language models\"; \"PTM-Psi models the structural... consequences\""},{"record_id":"rec_000116","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work presents a self-supervised patient encoder for diagnosis, not a generative model. Although it uses pretraining and retrieval, the abstract does not establish foundation-model-like broad pretraining or broad applicability.","evidence_snippet":"\"self-supervised, vision-only pretraining\"; \"genetic alignment via supervised contrastive loss\"; \"off-the-shelf retrieval capabilities\""},{"record_id":"rec_000117","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly frames the method as masked language modeling and reconstructing masked species, which is a generative mechanism. It does not provide evidence of broad pretraining or foundation-model-style generality.","evidence_snippet":"\"a Transformer-based JSDM that frames joint species distribution modeling as masked language modeling\"; \"reconstructing masked species\""},{"record_id":"rec_000118","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an adapter that turns existing DNA language models into RNA analyzers, so it is not a new generative model. The abstract emphasizes reduced pretraining needs rather than foundation-model-scale training or breadth.","evidence_snippet":"\"transforms DNA language models into effective RNA analyzers without RNA-specific pretraining\"; \"lightweight adapter\""},{"record_id":"rec_000119","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on explaining an existing transformer-based genome language model rather than introducing a new model. DNABERT-2 is described as a state-of-the-art genome language model, but the paper itself is an explanation study.","evidence_snippet":"\"apply it to the state-of-the-art gLM DNABERT-2\"; \"Transformer-based genome language model\""},{"record_id":"rec_000120","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"CellSTIC is a structured inference framework for cell-cell communication, not a generative architecture. The abstract focuses on interpretation of multimodal evidence and hierarchical representation, with no foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"resolves cell-cell communication\"; \"structured programs grounded in tissue architecture\"; \"hierarchical semantic representation\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0154.stderr.log:3165:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0045.stderr.log:1233:data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0030.stderr.log:135:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000233","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a machine-learning profiling framework for spatial ecotypes, not a generative architecture. No foundation-model-style pretraining or transfer evidence is stated.","evidence_snippet":"“machine-learning framework for multi-analyte profiling” and “identified nine SEs”"},{"record_id":"rec_000234","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The framework uses a large language model-based reasoner to interpret multimodal outputs, so a generative component is present. However, the abstract does not show foundation-model-like pretraining or broad transferable representation learning for the candidate system.","evidence_snippet":"“a large language model-based reasoner” and “chain-of-thought reasoning”"},{"record_id":"rec_000235","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly calls MAMMAL a foundation model and says it was pre-trained on 2 billion samples. It also states that it supports generative tasks on cross-modal inputs.","evidence_snippet":"“a foundation model for cross-modal learning,” “pre-trained on 2 billion samples,” and “supports ... generative tasks”"},{"record_id":"rec_000236","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a literature review of deep learning and explainable AI for ovarian cancer detection. It does not describe a candidate generative model or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“A Comprehensive Literature Review” and “This review synthesizes recent advances”"},{"record_id":"rec_000237","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"DeepSeMS is described as a transformer-based large language model that predicts chemical structures from gene clusters, which is generative. The abstract does not provide clear evidence of broad pretraining or other foundation-model-like characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"“transformer-based large language model” that “predicts secondary metabolite chemical structures”"},{"record_id":"rec_000238","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a protocol for using Geneformer rather than a new model paper. The abstract explicitly states Geneformer is pretrained on a large-scale corpus, but the described use is predictive and perturbative rather than generative.","evidence_snippet":"“Geneformer is a foundational artificial intelligence model” and “pretrained on a large-scale corpus of single-cell transcriptomes”"},{"record_id":"rec_000239","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract clearly describes a comprehensive review of RNA-binding proteins in neuroinflammation. It does not present a model, let alone a generative or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“This review systematically organizes” and “The review aims to construct a comprehensive theoretical framework”"},{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper studies LLM-based generation of visualization grammars, so a generative workflow is present. But it is an agentic authoring/application paper, and the abstract does not establish foundation-model-style pretraining for a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“vanilla LLM generation” and “All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output”"}]}
